Welsh members are afraid that they will "look like Muppets"
Some members of the Welsch assembly are disappointed with the suggestion that their title should change to MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament)
The reason for the proposal is plans to change the name of the assembly to the Welsh Parliament.
Members of gatherings across the political spectrum are concerned that the title could provoke ridicule.
One member on behalf of the Labour Party said his group was worried that "it rhymes with Twp and Pwp."
For readers outside Wales: In Welsh, twp means stupid and pwp means poop.
A member on behalf of the Plaid party said the group as a whole was "not happy" and proposed alternatives.
The Welsh Conservative stated that his group was approaching a “open-minded” name, but noted that MWP was close to Muppet.
In this context, the Welsh letter “w” is pronounced similarly to the letter “u” in Yorkshire English.
"The final decision on how members of the Welsh Assembly will be called will, of course, be up to the members themselves."
The Welsh Government Act of 2017 gave the Welsh Assembly the power to change its name.
In June, the commission published the results of a public meeting on proposals, where widespread support was given the option to call the assembly the Welsh Parliament.
When it comes to the title of the members of the assembly, the commission preferred the name Welsh Parliament Member, or WMP, but the MWP option received the most support in the public discussion.
The members of the assembly are clearly proposing alternative options, but the struggle to reach a consensus may bring concern to President Elin Jones, who is due to submit a draft legislation within a few weeks.
The reform laws will include further changes to the functioning of the assembly, including rules for disqualification of members and a proposal for a system of committees.
The members of the assembly will vote on the issue of their title as part of the discussion of the new legislation.
The Macedonians will vote on changing the country's name
On Sunday, voters will decide whether their country's name will change to "Republic of North Macedonia."
The popular vote should resolve a decades-long dispute with neighboring Greece, which has its own province called Macedonia.
Athens has long insisted that the name of its northern neighbor represents a claim on Greek territory, and has repeatedly protested against applications for the country's membership in the EU and NATO.
Macedonian President Gyorge Ivanov, who is an opponent of the plebiscite about the name change, said he would not follow the vote.
Proponents of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that changing the name is simply the price of joining the EU and NATO.
The bellows at St. Martin fall silent because churches in Harlem are struggling to survive
“The old people I’ve been talking to say there used to be a bar and a church on every corner,” Adams says.
"There's none there today."
According to him, the disappearance of bars is understandable.
“Today, people meet in a different way,” he says.
“Bars are no longer the living rooms of the neighborhood where people regularly go.”
When it comes to churches, he's worried that the money from selling the property won't be enough for as long as their officials expect, "and sooner or later they'll be exactly where they started."
As he adds, churches could be replaced by apartment buildings with flats full of this type of people who won't help the remaining sanctuaries in the neighborhood much.
"The vast majority of people who buy apartments in these houses will be white," he says, "and that's why the day will soon come when these churches will close altogether, because it's unlikely that most of these people who move into apartments would become members of churches."
Both churches built white congregations even before Harlem became a black metropolis – the Metropolitan Community in 1870, St. Martin ten years later.
The original White Methodist Congregation moved away in the 30's of the 20th century.
The building was taken over by a black congregation whose devotions took place nearby.
St. Martin took over the black congregation, led by Reverend John Howard Johnson, who was at the forefront of a boycott of retailers on 125th Street, the main shopping boulevard in Harlem, who refused to employ or elevate blacks.
The fire badly damaged the building in 1939, but when Father Johnson's parishioners were planning to rebuild it, they also ordered a chime.
Reverend David Johnson, the son of Johnson's father and his successor at St. Martin's, chimes proudly called the "bells of the poor."
The expert, who played the chime in July, called it differently: "cultural treasure" and "irreplaceable historical instrument."
This expert, Tiffany Ng of the University of Michigan, also noted that it is the world's first chimes played by black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who moved 18 years ago to a larger chime at a church in Riverside.
Mr. Merriweather says he wasn't replaced by St. Martin.
In the last few months, a complicated story of architects and builders took place near St. Martin, some of whom were summoned by lay representatives of the church, others by the episcopal diocese.
The Council of Elders – the leading body of the parish, consisting of lay representatives – sent a letter to the diocese in July expressing concerns that the diocese “will try to assert the costs” on the advice of the elders, although it did not take part in the hiring of the architects and builders sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners have complained about the lack of transparency from the diocese.
Shark injures 13-year-old boy diving for lobsters
According to an official report, the shark on Saturday attacked and injured a 13-year-old boy who was sinking for lobsters in California on the opening day of the lobster season.
The attack took place in the morning shortly before 7 a.m. near Beacon's Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV in San Diego that he had been sinking with his friends for about half an hour on Saturday morning when he overheard the boy calling for help, and then paddling with the whole group to help pull him out of the water.
Hammel stated that he at first considered the call to be the joy of a catch of lobster, but then "realized that he was screaming ‚Bitted me!
“He bited me!”
When Hammel got to the boy, he noticed that "his entire area of his collarbone was torn."
“I screamed for everyone to come from the water, ‘There’s a shark in the water!’” Hammel adds.
The boy was transported in critical condition by helicopter to the Children's Hospital in San Diego.
It is not known what kind of shark is behind the attack.
At a press conference at a press responsion, Larry Giles said the shark had been spotted in the area several weeks ago, but it was determined that it was not a dangerous species.
Giles added that the victim suffered traumatic wounds at the upper torso.
Authorities, due to investigations and safety, have closed access for 48 hours to the beach from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami's in Ecinitas.
Giles noted that more than 135 shark species live in the area, but most are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury's plans to enter UK cosmetics market
Sainsbury's wants to stand up to Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams chains with department store-style cosmetic aisles, where specialist saleswomen will be available.
As part of a strong entry into the UK cosmetics market, which is worth 2.8 billion pounds and continues to grow, while sales of fashion and homemakers are declining, cosmetics aisles will be tested in 11 stores across the country. In case of success, it will then expand to other stores next year.
The investment in cosmetics is coupled with efforts by supermarkets to find uses for shelves that were previously used for televisions, microwave ovens and household items.
Sainsbury's says it will double the size offer of beauty products up to 3,000 products, and for the first time include brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
More space will also be given to the existing assortment from L'Oreal, Maybelline and Burt's Bees, thanks to branded places similar to those in stores like Boots.
The supermarket is also reassigning its range of Boutique make-ups, so most of the products will be vegan, which young customers are increasingly demanding.
In addition, it will test the concessions of perfume retailer Fragrance Shop in two Saintsbury’s stores. The first such store opened last week in Croydon, south London, and another will be established this year in Selly Oak, Birmingham.
Shopping on the Internet and moving towards the daily purchase of a small amount of food in local stores means that supermarkets have to try harder to attract people.
Sainsbury's CEO Mike Coupe says stores will increasingly look like department stores as the supermarket chain struggles with a better range of services and non-food products to fight discount retailers like Aldi and Lidl.
Sainsbury's has opened hundreds of Argos stores and has also introduced a number of Habitat departments, which are the chains it bought two years ago. As a result, food sales have increased and acquisitions are thus even more profitable.
The supermarket's previous attempt to remake the department with the cosmetics and pharmacy assortment ended infamously.
At the start of the millennium, Sainsbury’s tested a joint venture with Boots, but the merger ended after an argument about distributinging the proceeds of drug sales in supermarkets.
The new strategy comes after Saintsbury’s sold its 281 pharmacies for 125 million pounds three years ago to Celesio, which owns the Lloyds pharmacy chain.
Lloyds plans to play its role by adding an expanded range of luxury skincare brands including La Roche-Posay and Vichy to four stores.
Sainsbury’s sales director Paul Mills-Hicks said: “We’ve changed the look and style of aisles with cosmetics to improve the environment for our customers.
We have also invested in specially trained colleagues that customers will be able to consult.
Our range of brands should meet all the needs and tempting environments, and a convenient location means that we are now introducing an attractive place to buy cosmetics that are changing the old ways of shopping.”
Peter Jones is "furious" after Holly Willoughby withdrew from her 11 million pound contract
Dragons Den star Peter Jones is "furious" after TV presenter Holly Willoughby withdrew from a $11-million-dollar contract with his lifestyle brand to focus on new contracts with Marks and Spencer and ITV
Willoughby doesn't have time for Truly's homemade clothing and accessories.
This couple's business has been likened to Gwyneth Paltrow's Goop brand.
Thirty-seven-year-old presenter of the show has announced her departure on Instagram.
Holly Willoughby infuriated Dragons Den star Peter Jones by abandoning their lucrative lifestyle business at the last minute – to concentrate on her new big contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
According to informed sources, Jones was "furious" when a television star during a tense Tuesday meeting at the headquarters of his business empire in Marlow, Buckinghamshir, admitting that because of the new contracts - worth up to 1.5 million pounders - she would no longer be able to dedicate enough time to their homemade clothing brand and Truly accessories.
The business has been likened to Goop by actress Gwyneth Paltrow and could double Willoughby's wealth at an estimated 11 million pounds.
When thirty-seven-year-old Willoughby announced on Instagram that she was abandoning the Truly brand, Jones set off from Britain for one of her summer settlements.
An unnamed source states: “Truly’s brand was the top priority for Holly.
It was supposed to be her long-term future, which will secure her for several decades in advance.
Her decision to leave completely shocked everyone involved.
No one on Tuesday could believe what was happening, the launch was so close.
There's a full warehouse of goods ready for sale at headquarters in Marlow.'
Experts believe that the departure of the presenter of This Morning, one of Britain's most proficient stars, could cost the company millions due to a fat investment in products from pillows and candles to clothes and home clothes, and a possible further delay in the brand's launch.
And it could mean the end of a long friendship.
Willoughby's three-childrien's mother, Dan Baldwin, have been close friends of Jones and his wife, Tara Capp, for a decade.
Willoughby founded the Truly brand with Tary Capp in 2016, and twenty-two-year-old Jones joined in March as chairman.
Couples go on holiday together and Jones has a 40% stake in Baldwin's television production firm.
Willoughby is to become M&S brand ambassador and replace Anta McPartlino in hosting I'm A Celebrity on ITV.
A source close to Jones said last night: "We will not comment on his business affairs."
Difficult acting “and then we fell in love”
He joked about the criticism he would receive from reporters as a note that some consider "unworthy of the president," and how positively he talks about the North Korean leader.
Why did President Trump give up so many things?
Trump responded with his mocking "announcing" voice.
“I didn’t give up anything.”
He noted that after their June first meeting in Singapore was praised by Trump as a big step towards the denuclearization of North Korea, Kim is interested in his next meeting.
But negotiations on the disposal of nuclear weapons were deadlocked.
More than three months after the June summit in Singapore, prominent North Korean diplomat Ri Yong Ho told world leaders at the U.N. General Assembly on Saturday that North Korea did not see an "adequate response" from the U.S. on its first steps to disarmament.
Instead, the U.S. is continuing sanctions aimed at increasing pressure.
In his speech, Trump expressed a much more optimistic view.
"We're doing great with North Korea," he said.
“We were on the brink of war with North Korea.
It would cost millions of lives.
We have a great relationship now.”
According to him, his efforts to improve relations with Kim have yielded positive results – an end to missile tests, assistance in liberating hostages and the return of the remains of U.S. troops to their homeland.
And he defended his unusual approach to informing about relations with Kim.
"It's so easy to act like a president, but instead of 10,000 people from outside trying to get into this crowded arena, we'd have about 200 people standing right here," Trump said, pointing to the crowd ahead.
A wave of tsunamis and earthquakes in Indonesia have devastated the island, killing hundreds
In the situation after the Lombok earthquake, for example, foreign NGOs have learned that they are not needed.
Although more than 10 percent of Lombok's population has been expelled from their homes, a national disaster has not been declared, a necessary precondition for speeding up international aid.
"In many cases, unfortunately, they have made it very clear that they do not require international assistance, so it is a bit challenging," Ms Sumbung says.
Save the Children puts together a team that travels to Palo, but it is not yet clear if foreign personnel can work here.
A spokesperson for the National Disasters Agency, Mr Sutupo, said Indonesian officials were evaluating the situation in Paul to determine whether international organizations would be allowed to help.
Since earthquakes occur very often in Indonesia, the earth is still woefully unprepared for the wrath of Mother Nature.
In Aceh, tsunami shelters have been built, but this is not at all common on other parts of the coast.
The loss of people's livies was also likely due to an apparent lack of sirens warning in Paulus about the tsunami, even when a warning was issued.
Even at the best of times, traveling among many islands in Indonesia is problematic.
In natural disasters, logistics is even more difficult.
The hospital ship, which docked in Lombok and assisted victims of the quake, is now moving to Paul, but it will take at least three days to reach the site of the new calamity.
Improving poor Indonesian infrastructure has been the main point of President Joko Widodo's election campaign, and the president has spent a lot of money on roads and railways.
He will seek elections again next year, but his government is plagued by shortfalls in funding.
President Joko is also facing pressure from persistent sectarian tensions in Indonesia, when members of the Muslim majority adopt a more conservative form of faith.
Stretches between Christians and Muslivs, in which gangs use machetes, bows and arrows and other primitive weapons, have already claimed the lives of more than 1,000 people and driven tens of thousands of people from their homes.
See: Daniel Sturridge of Liverpool scored a levelling goal against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge scored in the 89th minute at Stamford Bridge on Saturday and thus saved Liverpool from losing to Chelsea in the Premier League.
Sturridge got a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri about 27 metres in front of Chelsea's gate when his team lost 1-0.
He swung the ball to his left-hand and shot to the distant stick.
The shot flew high, pointing into the top right corner of the gate.
The ball eventually skipped Kepu Arrizabalaga and ended up in the net.
"I've only tried to get in the right place to get the ball, and players like Shaq are always playing as far ahead as possible, so I wanted to get as much time as possible," Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
"I've seen Kante approach me, so I didn't think much about it, and he shot at one touch."
Chelsea at half-time led 1-0 after a goal by Belgian star Eden Hazard in the 25th minute.
The Blues striker sended his passport back to Mateo Kovacic, then turned around in the middle of the pitch and sprinted in half of Liverpool.
Kovacic passed the ball quickly.
He then sent the ball through a beautiful aisle to Hazard into the limelight.
He escaped the defence and, with his left, finished to the distant pole alongside Alisson Becker of Liverpool.
On Wednesday at 15 o'clock, Liverpool will face the local team in the Champions League group at San Paolo stadium in Napoli, Italy.
Chelsea will play Thursday at 15 o'clock in London with Videoton in the UEFA European league.
The death toll from the tsunami in Indonesia has risen to 832
As the Indonesian Disasters Administration reported on Sunday morning, the death toll from earthquakes and tsunami waves climbed to 832.
The 7.5-degree earthquake struck on Friday, triggering waves of up to 6 metres highs and trapping many people in the wreckage of collapsed buildings, office spokesman Sutopo Purwo Nugroho told a news conference.
The city of Paula, which has over 380 thousand inhabitants, has been dotted with the ruins of collapsed buildings.
Police arrest thirty-three-year-old man suspected of stabbing woman
A woman's body was found in Birkenhead in Merseyside this morning. Police immediately launched a murder investigation.
A 44-year-old woman with stabbing injuries was found at 7.55 a.m. on Grayson Mews on John Street. On suspicion of murder, a 32-year-old man has been arrested.
Police urged people in the neighborhood who saw or heard something to turn themselves in.
Detective Inspector Brian O'Hagan said: "The investigation is just at the beginning, but I'd like to appeal to all those who moved near John Street in Birkenhead and saw or heard something suspicious to contact us.
I also ask everyone, especially taxi drivers, whose dashcams may have recorded something to contact us because they may have important information for our investigation.”
A police spokesperson confirmed that the dead woman lived in Birkenhead and the body was found in the building.
Friends arrived at the crime scene this afternoon who think they knew the woman to ask where she had been found this morning.
Police said an investigation is underway and they are now informing relatives of the victim.
The taxi driver, who lives in Grayson Mews, has just tried to return to his apartment, but police have told him that no one is allowed into or out of the building.
When he found out what had happened, he was unable to speak.
Residents have now learned that it will take several hours for them to be able to return home.
A police officer told one person that the whole area is now considered a crime scene.
A weeping woman appeared at the scene.
He repeats “it’s terrible.”
At 14 o'clock, there were two police vans behind the tape and another was waiting outside.
In a closed area stands a number of police officers who are monitoring the house.
Anyone who has any information can send a message to @MerPolCC, call line 101 or contact Crimestoppers anonymously at 0800 555 111, and provide code 247 of September 30.
Cromwell statue in parliament has recently become a hit due to an argument about "rewriting history"
His expulsion would be a well-deserved punishment for his fanatical Puritan followers destroying many English cultural and religious artifacts.
But the Cromwell Society called Mr Crick's proposal "follies" and "an effort to rewrite history."
Cromwell Society Chairman John Goldsmith said: "In the current debate on the removal of statues, it was inevitable that Oliver Cromwell's figure in front of the Palace of Westminster would become one of the goals.
Cromwell did not order or perform iconoclasm in English civil wars.
Perhaps the wrong Cromwell will be sacrificed for the actions of his predecessor Thomas in the previous century."
Cromwell's magnificent portrayal, authored by Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft, is a testament to the 19th-century view and part of the character's historiography, which many think still deserve to be celebrated.
"Many people consider Cromwell, though slightly less today than at the end of the 19th century, a defender of parliament from external pressure, in his case of course by the monarchy.
The accuracy of this interpretation is the subject of constant historical debates.
What is certain is that the mid-17th-century conflict shaped by the subsequent development of our nation, and Cromwell is a well-identified figure who represents one side of this divide.
His achievements as Lord Protector are also worth celebrating and remembering.”
Killing pig torn by Chinese farmer
According to information from local media, there was an attack and killing a farmer with a pig at a market in southeastern China.
According to the South China Morning Post on Sunday, a man who is referred to the surname “Yuan” was found dead with a severed artery in a pool of blood near a pigsty at a market in Liu-pishui in Guizhou province.
A pig breeder is preparing to administer a vaccine to pigs in a pig farm on 30 May 2005 in Xi'ning, Chinese Qinghai province.
He reportedly arrived on Wednesday with his cousin from the secondary province of Yunnan to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The following morning, his cousin found him dead and discovered that the door to the pigsty next door was open.
According to him, there was a large pork in the pigsty with a muzzle of blood.
According to the information, during a forensic examination, it was confirmed that a 250-kilogram pork tore the farmer.
"Cousin had his feet all bloody and torn," Guiyang Evening News quoted his cousin as referring to the surname "Wu."
On security camera footage can be seen as Yuan came to the market Thursday at 4.40 to feed his pigs.
His body was found an hour later.
The animal that killed the man did not belong to Yuan or his cousins.
The store manager told the Evening News that the pig was closed so that no one else would be attacked when police at the crime scene were gathering evidence.
Market officials are reportedly negotiating with Yuan's family for compensation for his death.
Swine attacks on humans are rare, but they have occurred in the past.
In 2016, a pig invaded a woman and her husband at a Massachusetts farm and the man was critically injured.
Ten years earlier, 300kg of a pig squeezed a Welsh farmer against a tractor and was chased away by the farmer's wife.
After an Oregon farmer ate its pigs in 2012, a farmer from Manitoba told CBC News that pigs aren't usually aggressive, but the taste of blood can act as a "trigger."
"They're just playing.
They're curious... they don't want to hurt you.
You just have to give them enough respect," he said.
Hurricane Rosa's remnants bring heavy rain to southwestern U.S
Hurricane Rosa is predicted to be fading and moving above the cooler waters of Mexico's northern coast.
Over the next few days, however, it will bring flood rains to northern Mexico and the southwest of the U.S.
At 5 a.m. eastern time, Rosa reached a wind speed of 137 km/h, which represents a Category 1 hurricane, and was located 620 km southeast of Punta Eugenia, Mexico.
Rosa is expected to move north on Sunday.
In the meantime, a low-pressure furrow is forming over the Pacific Ocean and shifting east to the U.S. west coast. As Rosa approaches the Baja California Peninsula on Monday, it will take the form of a tropical storm and begin to push tropical moisture north into the southwestern part of the U.S.
In some parts of Mexico, Rosa will deliver up to 250 mm of precipitation on Monday.
Tropical humidity will cause heavy rains in the southwest of the U.S. in the coming days.
Precipitation 25 to 100 mm locally causes dangerous torrential flooding, streams of debris and possible landslides in the desert.
Severe tropical humidity will cause rainfall to approach between 50 and 75 mm per hour, especially in southern Nevada and Arizona.
In some places in the southwestern U.S
With rapidly deteriorating conditions, torrential flooding may occur due to the scattered nature of tropical rain.
Due to the dangers of tropical collisions, it would be very unreasonable to walk into the desert.
Heavy rain can cause canyons to become raging rivers, and thunderstorms will bring local gusty winds and exploding dust.
An approaching low-pressure furrow will bring heavy local rainfall on some parts of the Southern California coast.
There may be total precipitation above 13 mm, which could cause smaller streams of debris and slippery roads.
It will be the first rainfall in this region.
On Sunday evening and Monday morning, rare tropical showers will start approaching Arizona, and on Monday night and Tuesday, rain will spread.
On Tuesday, heavy rain will spread to the Four Horns and will last until Wednesday.
In October, we can expect intense changes in temperatures throughout the U.S., as the Arctic begins to cool, but there will still be quite a lot of heat in the Tropical zone.
This sometimes leads to sharp temperature changes at short distances.
A great example of pronounced temperature differences is U.S
There's a difference of nearly 11 degrees Celsius between Kansas City in Missouri and Omaha in Nebraska, and between St. Louis and Des Moines in Iowa.
In the next few today, it will try again to expand the fading summer heat.
In most areas on Wednesday and in the eastern U.S
In New York City it could be 27°C on Tuesday, which is about 6 degrees above average.
Our long-term forecast shows a high probability of above-average temperatures in the eastern U.S. in the first half of October.
Brett Kavanaugh's interrogation was watched by more than 20 million people
More than 20 million people watched on six TV channels Thursday's eye-catching testimonies of Supreme Court nominees Brett Kavanaugh and Christine Blasey Ford, a woman who accused him of a sexual assault that allegedly occurred in the 1980s.
In the meantime, the political stalemate continued, and the announcers interrupted normal programs with reports of a sudden Friday twist: an agreement brokered by Arizona Senator Jeff Flake that the FBI would conduct a one-week investigation into the allegations.
Christine Ford told the Senate Judiciary Committee that she was 100% certain that Kavanaugh was groping her at a high school party while intoxicated and was trying to strip her clothes.
Kavanaugh, in his excited statement, said he's 100% sure it didn't happen.
It is likely that more than 20.4 million people watched the event, according to Nielsen.
The company counted average ratings on CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
Numbers for the other stations that broadcast statements, such as PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network, were not immediately available.
And Nielsen usually has trouble counting people looking at offices.
By comparison, it's a similar number of spectators watching football matches in a play-off or Oskar awards.
Fox News Channel, whose presenters strongly supported Kavanaugh's appointment, had the highest viewership of all stations when an average of 5.69 million viewers watched it during an all-day hearing, according to Nielsen.
The second was ABC with 3.26 million viewers.
Nielsen reports that CBS had 3.1 million, NBC 2.94 million, MSNBC 2.89 million and CNN 2.52 million viewers.
Interest persisted even after the end of negotiations.
The main character of Friday's drama was Senator Flake.
After the office of this moderate Republican issued a statement that he would vote for Kavanaugh, CNN and CBS cameras caught protesters shouting at him Friday morning as he prepared to take the elevator at a court committee hearing.
CNN vividly transmitted how for several minutes he stood with his eyes lowered and scolded.
One woman said, “I’m standing right in front of you.”
“Do you think he’s telling the truth?”
He also heard: “You have power, while many women are helpless.”
Flake said his office issued a statement, and even before closing the elevator door, he added that he would say more at the commission's meeting.
All cable and television networks were later broadcast live when the Judiciary Commission was supposed to vote on submitting Kavanaughove's nomination to vote to the entire senate.
But Flake said he would only do so if charges against the nominee by next week were examined by the FBI, which was demanded by a minority of Democrats.
Flake was partially persuaded by conversations with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
After an interview with Coons and several other senators, Flake made the decision.
Flake's vote was important because it was clear that without an investigation, Republicans wouldn't have enough votes to approve Kavanaugh.
President Trump has launched an FBI investigation into the allegations made against Kavanaugh.
Britain's Prime Minister May accuses critics of 'politicians' on Brexit
In an interview with the Sunday Times, Prime Minister Theresa May accused critics of her plan to leave the European Union for "politiciating" about Britain's future and undermining national interests.
British Prime Minister Theresa May arrives at the Conservative Party conference in Birmingham, September 29, 2018.
In another interview, which was close to that with the prime minister on the front page, her former foreign secretary Boris Johnson attacked her so-called plan from Chequers, saying the suggestion for Britain and the EU to levy their tariffs on each other was "utterly pointless."
Wayde Sims shooting: Police have arrested Dyteon Simpson, who is suspected of deating an LSU player
Police have arrested the suspect in the case of the shooting of Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old LSU player.
Baton Rouge's police department said Dyteon Simson, 20, had been arrested and imprisoned on second-degree murder charges.
Authoritians have released a video of the confrontation between Sims and Simpson and, according to police, Sims lost his glasses during the fight.
According to WABF, which belongs to CBS, police secured the glasses at the crime scene and found Simpson's DNA on them.
According to police after interrogation, Simpson confessed shooting Wayde.
According to the defense attorney, the bail was set at US$350 000.
On Friday, the coroner's office in East Baton Rouge released a preliminary report stating that the cause of death was a gunshot wound to the head and neck.
Thanks the Louisiana refugee police unit, the state police's crime laboratory, the Southern University police and local citizens for assisting in an investigation that led to an arrest.
LSU sports director Joe Alleva thanked local police officers for "diligence and seeking justice."
Sims was 20 years old.
The two-foot striker grew up in Baton Rouge, where even his father Wayne played basketball for LSU,
He averaged 5.6 points and 2.6 rebounds per game last season.
LSU basketball coach Will Wade said Friday morning that Wayde's death "shaken" the team and "shocked" him.
"This is what you're still afraid of," Wade said.
Volcano spews ashes on Mexico City
The ashes rolling from the Popacatépetl volcano hit the southern districts of Mexico's capital.
The National Disasters Prevention Center on Saturday warned Mexicans not to approach the volcano, after volcanic activity increased and within 24 hours, 183 gas and ash emissions were recorded.
The center watched a series of roars and aftershocks.
On social media, images of car windshields covered with a thin layer of ash appeared in some neighborhoods of Mexico City, such as Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have seen an increase in the activity of the volcano, located 72 kilometers (45 miles) southeast of the capital. The latest quake rocked central Mexico in September 2017 and had a strength of 7.1 degrees.
Vulcano known as "Don Goyo" has been active since 1994.
Before the anniversary of the independence vote, police clashed with Catalan separatists
Six people were arrested in Barcelona on Saturday after protesters for independence clashed with riot police and thousands of people took part in competing demonstrations on the occasion of the first anniversary of the independence vote, which divided Catalonia.
Riot police officers blocked a group of masked separatists throwing eggs and powder paint at them. The one on the streets, which are usually flooded with tourists, created dark clouds of dust.
Later, scuffles broke out, to which the police had to use batons.
Within a few hours, groups supporting independence sang “We won’t forget, we won’t forgive” in the face of protesting supporters of the union who called out “Long live Spain.”
According to the local press, fourteen people were lightly injured in protests.
A year after the October 1 referendum, which Madrid considers illegal but separatist Catalans celebrate, tensions remain high in a region seeking independence.
Voters voted overwhelmingly for independence, but turnout in elections was low because opponents of detachment boycotted them.
According to Catalan authorities, nearly 1,000 people were injured last year after police in violent clashes across the region tried to end votes at polling stations.
Groups promoting independence remained outside overnight on Friday to prevent a demonstration in support of the national police.
The demonstration took place, but had to choose a different route.
Sixty-eight-year-old Electricist Narcis Termes, who took part in a separatist protest along with his wife, said he was no longer hoping for the prospect of independence for Catalonia.
“Last year we had some of the best moments.
My parents wept with joy that they could vote, but now we're at an impasse," he said.
Despite an important, albeit close victory in regional elections last December, Catalan parties supporting independence had to fight to keep things going this year. Many of their best-known leaders have either gone into exile themselves or are awaitinging in custody for their participation in organising a referendum and subsequently declaring independence.
Forty-two-year-old assembler Joan Puig recorded a protest in support of police on his phone, saying the conflict was stirred up by politicians on both sides.
“The situation is getting tighter,” he adds.
Oriol Junqueras, one of nine Catalan leaders who have been awaiting trial since the end of last year, announced on Saturday that he would run for the European Parliament next year.
"Candidacy in European elections is the best way to condemn the decline in democratic values and repression we are witnessing by the Spanish government," he said.
Londonderry: Men jailed after car crash into house
Three men, aged 33, 34 and 39, were imprisoned after they repeatedly crashed their car into a house in Londonderry.
The event happened at Ballynagard Crescent on Thursday at approximately 19:30 local time.
Detective Inspector Bob Blemmings said there had been damage to the gate and the house itself.
At one point, someone may also have shot at the car.
Mengo's shot secured Livingstone's 1-0 victory over Rangers
Dolly Menga's first goal for Livingston secured victory
Livingston, who progressed from the lower division, shocked Rangers and awarded Steven Gerrard only his second defeat from 18 games as Ibrox club manager.
Dolly Menga's hit proved decisive when Gary Holt's team reached Hibernian in second place.
Gerrard's team has yet to win in the Premiership this season on the guests' pitch and will face the first Hearts next Sunday, on which they will lose eight points.
Even before that, Rangers will host Rapid Vienna in the Europa League on Thursday.
Livingston has extended a number of unbeaten matches in the division to six appearances, so head coach Holt, who replaced Kenny Miler last month, has yet to experience defeat.
Livingston didn't take a chance against lackluster guests
Holt's team should have led long before the goal, because his directness was causing Rangers a lot of problems.
Scott Robinson penetrated the defence, but failed in front of the gate, then Alan Lithgow narrowly missed Craig Halkett's header in front of goal.
It was enough to watch the Rangers play in front of them because they knew they could torment the guests with standard situations.
And so came the key goal.
Against Rangers, a free-kick was ordered and Livingston formed a loophole, Declan Gallagher and Robinson together sent Menga, who the ball flowed and scored in the middle of the penalty area.
At this stage, Rangers dominated possession of the ball, but the home defence was impenetrable for them, and goalie Liam Kelly mostly didn't have much to do.
The same picture of the game was also in the second half, although Alfredo Morelos forced Kelly to intervene.
Scott Pittman was halted by Rangers goalie Allan McGregor's feet and Lithgow missed from another standard Livingstone situation.
There were constantly centres flying into Livingston's penalty area, which the players were constantly cleaning up, and two penalty kicks also went to waste - after Halkett's attack on substitute Glenn Middleton and another by hand.
“Phenomenon” from Livingston – analysis
Alasdair Lamont from BBC Scotland at Tony Macaroni Arena Stadium
Phenomenal performance and result for Livingston.
They have been outstanding without exception and continue to exceed expectations on their way up.
Their style of play and team have hardly changed since returning to the top, but Holt deserves great praise for how he has roused the team since his arrival.
He's had so many heroes.
Captain Halkett was tremendous, leading a superbly arranged defense, while Menga kept Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall on standby all the time.
Rangers are lacking inspiration.
They used to be good under Gerrard, but they don't reach those standards anymore.
Their ending was poor – only once penetrated the home side – and for Rangers it should be a wake-up call because they are located in the middle of the table.
Erdogan received a mixed reception in Cologne
When the leaders of Turkey and Germany met on Saturday (September 29) for breakfast in Berlin, the sky was blue and everyone was smiling.
It is the last day of President Erdogan's controversial visit to Germany, which aims to remedy relations between NATO allies.
They disagreed over a number of topics, including human rights, press freedom and Turkey's accession to the EU.
Erdogan then headed to Cologne, where he opened a huge new mosque.
In this city, Turkey's largest population is outside Turkey.
Police, for security reasons, prevented a crowd of 25,000 people from meeting in front of the mosque, but there were many supporters in the neighborhood who wanted to see their president.
Hundreds of people were also heard – many of whom were Kurds – protesters against Erdogan, who condemned his policies and the German government’s decision to welcome him to the country.
Opposing protests reflect the controversy of a visitor whom some German Turks hailed as a hero, and others cursed like an autocrat.
Deptford accident: Cyclist died after car collision
A cyclist has died in London after a collision with a car.
The crash occurred at approximately 10:15 a.m. local time, close to the intersection of Bestwood Street with the bustling Evelyn Street in Deptford in the southeast of the city.
The driver of the car stopped and an ambulance soon arrived, but the man was dead at the scene.
The death occurred just months after another cyclist died about a kilometre and a half from the site of Saturday's tragedy in an accident on Childers Street, when the driver fled the scene.
Metropolitan police said officers were now trying to identify the man and inform his relatives.
The road is closed, buses run on replacement routes and motorists are advised to avoid the area.
Long Lartin prison: Six guards injured in riots
According to the prison office, six guards were injured in riots in a high-security men's prison.
The riots erupted at Long Lartin in Worcestershire on Sunday around 9:30 a.m. local time and are still ongoing.
To handle the riots involving eight prisoners in one wing, specialized guards from the "Tornado" team were called.
The wardens suffered minor injuries and were treated at the scene.
A spokesman for the prison service said: "Specially trained prison workers have been deployed to cope with an ongoing incident in Long Lartin.
Six members of the staff were treated with injuries.
We do not tolerate violence in our prisons, and it is clear that those responsible will be handed over to the police and can spend a longer time behind bars.”
There are more than 500 inmates at Long Lartin prison, including some of the country's most dangerous offenders.
In June, it was reported that the prison's director had ended up after an attack by a prisoner in hospital.
And last October, riot squads were summoned to the prison due to serious riots in which personnel were attacked with billiard balls.
Hurricane Rosa threatens the floods Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City (areas where there's drought can benefit)
It's not usual for Arizona to be hitted by a tropical low-pressure furrow, but that's exactly what's likely to happen early next week, when Hurricane Rosa's remaining energy outscores the desert southwest and carries the risk of torrential flooding.
The National Meteorological Service has already issued flood warnings for Monday and Tuesday from western Arizona for southern and eastern Nevada, southeastern California and Utah, including cities like Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to make a direct journey through Phoenix on Tuesday and bring rain on Monday night.
"Since 1950, only ten tropical cyclones have retained tropical storm or pressure lower status within 320 km of Phoenix!
Katrina (1967) was a hurricane still 64 km from the border of Arizona."
The latest models of the National Hurricane Center predict precipitation of 50 to 100 mm, in the Mogollon Rim area in Arizona sometimes up to 150 mm.
In other areas of the desert nortwest, including the center of the Rocky Mountains and the Great Basin, 25 to 50 mm is likely to fall, with local totals of up to 100 mm.
For those not at risk of torrential flooding, Rosa's rain can be a blessing as the area has been affected by drought.
Flooding is a very serious thing, but these rainfalls may help somewhere, because the southwestern U.S. has just been hit by drought.
According to U.S. Drought Monitor, more than 40% of Arizona experiences at least “extreme drought,” the second-highest category.
The path of Hurricane Rosa will first lead to the Baja California peninsula in Mexico.
Rosa, still with a hurricane strength with a maximum wind speed of 137 km/h, then on Sunday morning will be 620 km south of Punta Eugenia in Mexico and will move at 19 km/h to the north.
Then the storm encounters the cooler waters of the Pacific Ocean and therefore loses strength.
It is expected to arrive in Mexico Monday afternoon or evening as a tropical storm.
In some parts of Mexico, precipitation will be strong, posing a great risk of flooding.
"In an area from Baja California to northwestern Sonora, total rainfall of 75 to 150 mm is expected, while it can occur up to 250 mm," according to weather.com.
Rosa then travels like a tropical storm through Mexico to the north and arrives at the Arizona border in the early hours of Tuesday morning. She then passes through Arisona and enters southern Utah late Tuesday night.
“It is expected that the main risk of Rosa or its remnants will be very heavy rainfall in Baja California, northwestern Sonora and the desert southwest of the U.S.,” the National Hurricane Center reported.
These rains are expected to cause deadly torrential flooding and streams of debris in deserts and landslides in mountainous terrain.
