Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia have killed hundreds of people and the death toll continues to rise
Authorities announced on Saturday that at least 384 people lost their lives after the Indonesian island Sulawesi was hit by large-scale earthquakes.
Hundreds of people gathered at a festival on the beach in Palu on Friday. At dusk, however, the coast was hit by up to six-meter waves, which killed many people and destroyed everything that stood in their way.
The tsunami was preceded by a 7.5-degree earthquake.
"When the threat of a tsunami emerged yesterday, people were still continuing activities on the beaches and didn't flee immediately, so they fell victim to the waves," BNPB's spokesman Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for the BNPB disaster mitigation, said at a briefing in Jakarta.
"The tsunami didn't go by itself, taking cars, logs, houses, hitting everything on the ground," Nugroho said, adding that before hitting the coast, the tsunami on the open sea was spreading at 800 km/h.
He stated that some people had escaped to the trees before the wave and survived.
About 16,700 people were evacuated to 24 centers in Palo.
Aerial photographs released by the disaster mitigation agency show many destroyed buildings and shops, twisted and collapsed bridges and a mosque surrounded by water.
The aftermath also rocked the coastal city on Saturday.
A series of earthquakes have been smelled in an area where 2.4 million people live.
The Indonesian Technology Evaluation and Application Agency (BPPT) said in a statement that the energy released by Friday's powerful aftershocks was approximately 200 times greater than that of an atomic bomb overthrown in World War II on Hiroshima.
The city lies at the end of a long, narrow bay, which may have magnified the tsunami.
Nugroho described the damage as "extensive" and said thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels had collapsed.
He mentioned that the bodies of some of the victims had been found wedged under the debris of collapsed buildings, adding that 540 people had been injured and 29 were missing.
Nugroho informed that the number of casualties and damages may be greater on the coast 300 km north of Pal, in an area called Donggala, which is close to the epicentre of an earthquake.
According to him, the communication was "completely crippled without any information" from Donggala.
The Red Cross said in a statement that more than 300 000 people live in the area, adding that its workers and volunteers were headed to the affected areas.
"It's a tragedy, but it could get a lot worse," the statement said.
The agency received considerable criticism on Saturday for failing to inform that Pala had been hit by a tsunami, although authorities said the waves came at a time when warnings had been issued.
In amateur footage shared on social media, a man on the upper floor of the building can be heard desperately shouting people on the street from an impending tsunami.
Within a few minutes, the coast will be hit by a water that carries away buildings and cars.
Reuters was unable to verify the footage immediately.
Earthquakes and a tsunami caused a major power outage that interrupted communications around Palu, making it difficult for authorities to coordinate rescue efforts.
According to the authorities, the army began sending cargo planes with help from Jakarta and other cities, but evacuated people still urgently need food and other basic necessities.
The city's airport has been reopened only for aid transportation and will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo was scheduled to visit the evacuation centers in Palu on Saturday.
The death toll from the tsunami in Indonesia has climbed to more than 800.
It's too bad.
When World Vision workers from Donggala were arriving in the city of Paul, where employees were hiding in shelters from cells built in the yard of an office building, they saw images of doom along the way, Mr Doseba says.
"They said they've seened a lot of destroyed houses," he adds.
It's too bad.
When auxiliary groups began a sad activity of disaster relief, some of them complained that foreign workers with great experience were not allowed to come to Paul.
According to Indonesian regulations, funding, supplies and personnel from abroad can only start flowing into place of the calamity only when a national disaster zone is proclaimed.
That hasn't happened yet.
"It's still a disaster at the provincial level," says Aulia Arriani, a spokesman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
“Once the government says ‘OK, this is a national disaster,’ we can launch international aid, but there isn’t such a situation yet.”
When the second night came in Paulo after Friday's earthquake and tsunami, friends and relatives of those who are still missing were still hoping that their loved ones would be those miracles that lightened the grim stories of natural disasters.
On Saturday, they pulled a little boy from the canal.
On Sunday, paramedics freed a woman who had been imprisoned for two days under debris next to his mother's body.
Gendon Subandono, coach of Indonesia's paragliding team, coached two of the missing paragliders to the Asian Games, which ended this month in Indonesia.
Another of the people confined to the Roa Roa Hotel, including Mr. Mandagi, were his students.
“As a person who has been involved in paragliding for a long time, I have my own emotional burden,” she says.
Mr. Gendon recounted how in the hours that followed after the paragliding community learned about the collapse of the Roa Roa Hotel, he desperately send messages via WhatsApp to contestants from Palu who were attending the beach festival.
His news, however, instead of a pair of blue ticks, ended with a gray marker.
"I think it means the messages weren't delivered," he says.
