The Macedonians vote in a referendum on whether they want to change the country's name
The people of Macedonia on Sunday voted in a referendum on whether the country's name should change to "Republic of North Macedonia," which would resolve a decades-old controversy with Greece, which is blocking applications for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece has a province called Macedonia, claiming that the name of its northern neighbor represents a claim to its territory, and has therefore vetoed Macedonia's accession to NATO and the EU.
Both governments reached an agreement in June on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents argue that the change will weaken the ethnicity of Macedonia's majority Slavic population.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he would not vote in the referendum, and a campaign calling for a boycott raised doubts about whether at least 50 percent of voters would appear needed to make the referendum valid.
“Are you in favor of membership of NATO and the EU accepting an agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, say it's a price worth paying for making Macedonia, one of the successor countries of the former Yugoslavia. could join organizations such as the EU and NATO.
"I came today to vote for the future of the country, for the young people in Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the roof of the European Union, because it means a safer life for all of us," says 79-year-old Olivera Georgievska of Skopje.
While the referendum is not binding, enough members of parliament have said they will follow the outcome of the vote.
Changing the title would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The State Electoral Commission said there had been no problems reported within 13 hours.
Voter turnout, however, was just 16 percent compared to 34 percent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66 percent of registered voters cast their ballot.
“I went to vote because of my children, our place is in Europe,” says Gjose Tanevski, a 62-year-old voter from the capital, Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and son Dushko are handing over their votes in a Macedonian referendum on a name change that would open the way to NATO and the European Union in Strumica, Macedonia on September 30, 2018.
In front of parliament in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepares a small podium and pulls chairs in front of tents erected by people who boycott the referendum.
“We are for NATO and the EU, but we want to enter them with our heads held high, not the door for servants,” Kavadarkov said.
“We are a poor country, but we have our pride.
If they don't want us like Macedonia, we can turn to others like China and Russia and become part of Eurasian integration."
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership will bring Macedonia, which has an unemployment rate in excess of 20%, much-needed investment.
"I think the vast majority will be in favour because more than 80 percent of our citizens are for the EU and NATO," Zaev said after voting.
According to him, the “yes” result would be a “confirmation of our future.”
According to a survey released last Monday by the Macedonian Institute for Policy Research, between 30 and 43 percent of voters could take part in the referendum – less than the required turnout.
In another poll organized by the Macedonian station Telma TV, it was found that 57 percent of respondents intended to vote on Sunday.
Of those, 70 percent said they would vote yes.
In order for the referendum to be successful, it must be attended by 50 percent plus one voter.
The failure of the referendum would mark the first serious blow to pro-Western government politics since it took place last May.
Look: Sergio Aguero from Manchester City went for a goal through Brighton's entire defence
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling handled Brighton's defence on Saturday, securing Manchester City a 2-0 win at Etihad stadium in Manchester.
