The Labour Party is no longer a diverse current of opinion.
He's intolerant of those who express their opinions
When the Momenta activists in my local party voted for my admonition, it was no surprise.
After all, I am the last of a number of Labour MPs to whom they have said they are not welcome – because they sincerely gave their opinion.
My colleague in Parliament, Joan Ryan, was treated similarly because she resolutely opposed anti-Semitism.
In my case, they criticised me in admonition that I didn't agree with Jeremy Corbyn.
Regarding the importance of a responsible economic policy, on national security, on Europe, paradoxically similar issues on which Jeremy disagreed with previous leaders.
In an announcement of Friday's Labour Party meeting in Nottingham East, it said that "we want to make meetings accessible and productive."
Most of the eight years since I've been a local member of parliament on behalf of the Labour Party, the GC meetings on Friday night were exactly like that.
Unfortunately, most of the meetings do not take place nowadays, and the promise of a “kinder, kinder” policy has long since been forgotten, if it has ever begun.
It is becoming increasingly apparent that different perspectives aren't being tolerated in the Labour Party, and every opinion is being judged on whether it is acceptable to party leadership.
It started shortly after Jeremy became the leader. The colleagues I had previously thought had similar political views were suddenly expecting me to turn 180° and hold positions I would not otherwise be able to agree with – whether it’s national security or the EU’s single market.
Whenever I speak in public – and it doesn’t even matter what I say – it is followed by the tirade of social media abuses, where they call for my dismissal, they condemn the politics of the center and tell me that I should not be in the Labour Party.
And it's not just my experience.
I know I'm more lucky than some of my colleagues, because the comments aimed at me are usually political.
I have respect for the professionalism and determination of those, colleagues, who face a flurry of sexist or racist insults every day, but will never back down.
One of the aspects of this era of politics, from which I am the most disappointed, is how insults become the norm.
Jeremy Corbyn said last week that the Labour Party should cultivate a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer the diverse current of opinion, and with every proposal to express distrust or change the rules of choice, the party becomes more limited.
In the last two years, I've been advised many times to hold back, don't hear back so much, and then "I'll be fine."
But that's not why I went into politics.
Since I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago, provoked by the negligence of Margaret Thatcher's government, which has let my class of the general school literally disintegrate, I've struggled for better public services for those who need them the most - whether as a local councillor or minister of government.
I've never hid my politics, even at the last election.
No one in Nottingham East could have been surprised by my political positions and areas in which I disagree with the current leadership.
To those who supported the proposal on Friday, all I would say is that when a country breaks towards Brexit, which damages households, businesses and our public services, I don't send the desire to waste time and energy on my loyalty to the Labour Party leader.
My main message, however, is not addressed to the Moment in Nottingham, but to my constituents, whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I am proud to serve you, and I promise that no threats of appeal or political pragmatism will deter me from acting according to what, in my opinion, the best interest of all of you.
Chris Leslie is a member of parliament for Nottingham East
