Two late fives misrepresented the final result a bit, but there's no doubt Ayr deserved to triumph in this amazingly entertaining Tennent's Premiership match.
Now they're at the head of the table, like the only undefeated.
In the end, it was an excellent defense and a better use of the chances that helped the homers win, and coach Peter Murchie had every right to be satisfied.
"We've been through great trials in our games and we're still unbeaten, so I have to be happy," he says.
Robyn Christie of Melrose says: “All the honor of Ayra, they seized the odds better than us.”
Grant Anderson's five in the 14th minute, converted by Frazier Clim, sent Ayr forward, but a yellow card for Scottish representative Rory Hughes, who was vacated by the Warriors for the match, allowed Melrose to take advantage of numerical superiority and Jason Baggot scored an unmatched five.
Climo increased Ayra's lead with a penalty and then scored exactly at half-time and converted a solo attempt, thus adjusting to 17-5 for Ayr.
But Melrose started the second half well well and Patrick Anderson's five, converted Baggot, cut the lead to five points.
This was followed by a longer delay due to a serious injury by Ruaridh Knott, who had left the pitch on a stretcher, and when the game started again, Ayr jumped again thanks to Stafford McDowall's fiver, which was transformed by Climo.
Representing captain Ayra Blair Macpherson then received a yellow card and Melrose once again took advantage of the player thanks to Bruce Colvino's unchanged five after a brief strong pressure.
However, the homers returned, and when Struan Hutchinson got yellow for attacking Clim without the ball, MacPherson from the penalty line-up placed the ball behind advancing maul Ayra.
Climo converted, again almost from the start, after Kyle Rowe grabbed David Armstrong's kick and sent wing Gregor Herne for the fifth-ranked homer.
Still Game stars start a new career in hospitality
It appears that Still Game star Ford Kieran is moving into the hospitality industry. He has been found to have been appointed director of a licensed restaurant company.
The sixty-six-year-old actor plays Jack Jarvis on the BBC's popular show, in which he stars alongside longtime comedy partner Greg Hemphill.
The pair announced that the upcoming ninth season would be the final season of the series, and Kiernan appears to be already planning his life after Craiglang.
According to an extract from the official register, he is the director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor declined to comment on the report, but a Scottish Sun source has hinted that Kiernan wants to get involved in Glasgow's "prosperous restaurant business."
‘The sea is ours’: inland Bolivia hopes the court will reopen its way to the Pacific Ocean
Sailors are patrolled in front of Naval headquarters in La Paz, which is decorated with cable cars.
Above the public buildings flies a blue flag in the color of the ocean.
The nautical bases from Lake Titicaca for the Amazon are described by the motto: "The sea is rightly ours.
Obtaining it is our duty.”
There is still a vivid memory throughout inland Bolivia on the coast that the country has lost in favor of Chile in a bloody war for resources in the 19th century – as well as the desire to plough the Pacific Ocean again.
These desires are perhaps the largest in several decades as Bolivia awaits a ruling by the International Court of Justice, which is expected to fall on October 1 after five years of deliberation.
"Bolívia has an energy, a spirit of unity and tranquility and, of course, expects a positive result," says Bolivian diplomat Roberto Calzadilla.
Many Bolivians will follow MSD's decisions on large screens across the country with the hope that the tribunal in The Hague will decide in favor of Bolivia's assertion that, after many decades of intermittent talks, Chile is obliged to negotiate that it will give Bolivia a sovereign approach to the sea.
Charismatic Aboriginal President Evo Morales – who has a controversial battle to re-electe next year – is also very interested in Monday’s decision.
"We're very close to going back to the Pacific Ocean," he declared at the end of August.
But some analysts believe it is unlikely that the court will rule in favor of Bolivia – and even if it did, not much would change.
This U.N. body, based in the Netherlands, has no power to assign Chilean territory to anyone and said it would not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that MSD's decision comes just six months after the latest arguments were made shows that the case "wasn’t complicated,” as Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law, says.
And for the last four years, not only didn't they support Bolivia's cause, but they may have hindered it.
"The issues of access to the sea have been seized by Bolivia's incumbent government," Zárate says.
He adds that Morales' offensive rhetoric has weakened Chile's remaining goodwill.
Bolivia and Chile will take action again someday, but it will be extremely difficult to have discussions after this.
Both countries haven't exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, a representative of Bolivia in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court's decision-making was unusually swift.
Monday will bring Bolivia "an exceptional opportunity to start a new era of relations with Chile" and the chance to "end 139 years of disagreement with mutual advantages," he said.
Calzadilla also denied that Morales – still one of the most beloved presidents in Latin America – would use the issue of the sea as a political crutch.
"Bolivia will never give up its right to have access to the Quiet Ocean," he added.
“Verdiction is an opportunity to find that we need to deal with the past.”
North Korea says there won't be nuclear disarmament unless the U.S
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong says his nation will never disarm its nuclear weapons first if it can't trust Washington
Ri spoke at the U.N. General Assembly on Saturday.
He called on the United States to make vows at a summit of the leaders of the two rivals in Singapore.
His comment comes after U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears to be preparing to restart nuclear diplomacy, which has become deadlocked, for more than three months for a Singapore meeting with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un.
Ri says it's "hopeful" that ongoing sanctions and U.S. opposition to the declaration ending the Korean War could sometimes bring the North to its knees.
Washington is cautious about agreeing to the declaration if Pyongyang doesn't take significant steps to disarmament at first.
Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second
There is, however, general skepticism that Pyongyang is serious about renouncing this arsenal, which the country probably sees as the only way to ensure its security.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for Kim-Trump's second summit.
Paris parades show that the latest trend of huge headgear will soon arrive at your fashion stores
If you want to expand your hat collection or completely block the sun, your search is over.
Valentino and Thom Browne have unveiled a series of crazy huge headgears for their spring/Summer 2019 collections at a parade that captivated everyone at Paris Fashion Week.
Very impractical hats have flooded Instagram this summer, and these designers have sent their exciting creations to the catwalk.
An exhibition piece from Valentino was an exaggerated beige hat with a wide edge resembling feathers that flooded the heads of models.
Other oversized accessories were adorned melons, a witch's hat and even pineapple - but they're not meant to warm their heads.
Thom Browne also introduced a selection of bizarre masks – and just in time for Halloween.
Many of the multi-colored masks have sewn-up lips and resemble Hannibal Lecter rather than high fashion.
One creation resembled diving equipment even with snorkel and goggles, while another looked like a cone with melted ice cream.
And if you continue with great fashion – you’re lucky.
Fashion experts are predicting that huge caps could reach fashion stores as well.
Overgrown hats come after "La Bomba," a straw with a half-metre-wide krempa worn by everyone from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The iconic brand behind this very impractical hat that flooded with social media sent another huge creation to the catwalks – a straw beach bag that’s almost as big as the model in a swimsuit carrying it.
A dark orange raffi bag lined with raffi fringes and equipped with a white leather handle at Paris Fashion Week was an exhibition piece of the La Riviera Spring/Summer 2019 collection from Jacquemus.
Celebrity stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “I expect big hats and beach bags to appear in fashion stores for next summer – given that the designer has had such great success with them, it would hardly be possible to ignore the demand for excessively large accessories.”
John Edward: Language skills are a necessity for global citizens
Scottish independent schools have had a number of academic achievements, which continues in 2018 with other excellent exam results. Individual and group achievements in sports, artistic, musical and other community activities only underscore this.
These schools have more than 30,000 pupils throughout Scotland, are represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) and strive to provide their pupils and parents with the best possible level of service.
Independent schools prepare their pupils for further and higher education, the career they have chosen, and their place as global citizens.
As an education sector that can design and implement individually tailored school education plans, we observe that modern languages are still popular and desirable subjects in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: "If you speak to a man in a language he understands, it's going right into his head.
If you're talking to him in his own language, it's right in his heart."
It is an important observation that if we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries, we cannot rely only on English.
The latest results of this year's exams show that languages come first with tables, with independent schools having the highest success rates.
In total, 68 percent of pupils who learned a foreign language achieved the best grade of A.
Data collected from 74 SCIS member schools show that 72 percent of Mandarin students, 72 percent of German students, 69 percent of French students and 63 percent of Spanish students achieved the highest grade of A.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as important skills that children and youth will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages such as the subjects chosen by the pupils are as esteemed in the study plans of independent and other schools as STEM (sciences, technologies, technical disciplines and mathematics).
A survey carried out by Britain's Employment and Skills Commission in 2014 showed that, for the reasons given by employers as difficulties in filling vacancies, 17 per cent are insufficient language skills.
Therefore, language skills are increasingly becoming a necessity in preparing young people for their future careers.
As more and more job opportunities require languages, these skills are essential in a globalised world.
Whether one chooses a career, if one has learned a second language, it will have the real advantage of having such a lifelong skill in the future.
Thanks to the ability to directly communicate directly with people from foreign countries, a person who speaks multiple languages automatically gains an advantage over the competition.
According to a survey conducted by YouGov in 2013 among 4,000 adults in the UK, 75 per cent of them failed to speak a foreign language well enough to have a conversation, with the only language spoken by a double-digital percentage of people, specifically 15%, was French.
That's why it's important for today's children to invest in language learning.
If children know multiple languages, especially emerging-employment languages, they will have a better chance of finding meaningful jobs.
In Scotland, each school differs in what languages it teaches.
A number of schools focus on more classic modern languages, while others teach languages that consider themselves the most important for the UK in the outlook for 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Whatever the child is interested in, he/she will always have a choice in independent schools from several languages with teachers who are experts in the field.
Scotch independent schools are dedicated to ensuring an educational environment that prepares children and equips them with the skills necessary for success, no matter what awaits them in the future.
At the moment, there is no denying that languages are necessarily important for the future of the country in the global business environment, so this has to be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should be regarded as “international communication skills.”
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien le faire.
John Edward is the director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
