Trump's performance in West Virginia
President Donald Trump will launch a wave of campaign appearances tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
It's the first of five meetings scheduled for next week, including stops at friendly venues such as Tennessee and Mississippi.
Due to the suspension of the vote to confirm his nominee's vacancy in the Supreme Court, Trump wants to win support for the upcoming mid-term election as Republicans face the risk of losing Congress in the November election.
At what time does Trump's performance begin tonight and how can you watch him online?
Trump's rally in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 19:00 Eastern time, today Saturday, September 29, 2018.
You can watch Trump's rally in West Virginia online below via a live stream on YouTube.
Trump is likely to be talking about Brett Kavanaugh's high-team court hearing, which took place this week and has been very tense because of accusations of inappropriate sexual behavior. The Senate's confirmation vote is expected to be postponed by a weekhone before an FBI investigation takes place.
But the main goal of this wave of rallies is to help Republicans pick up pace before the November elections.
According to Reuters, campaign organizers President Trump says these five rallies next week aim to "give energy to volunteers and supporters so that Republicans can try to protect and expand the majority they have in the Senate and House of Representatives."
"Checking over the Senate is so important to his agenda that the president will go to as many states as possible in this busy season of campaigning," a Trump campaign spokesperson, who did not wish to be named, told Reuters.
According to Westirian Metro News, supporters from "Ohio and Pennsylvania could come to today's rally at Wesbanco Arena in Wheeling and could be tracked by Pittsburgh media."
On Saturday it will be the second time in the last month that Trump will visit West Virginia, in which he scored more than 40 percentage points in 2016.
Trump is trying to help the Republican candidate for the West Virginia Senate, Patrick Morrisey, who is lagging behind in the polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come to help him in the polls,” said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at the University of West Virginia.
