North Korea says it will "in no way" disarm unilaterally and without trust
The Secretary of Foreign Affairs of North Korea said Saturday that continued U.N. sanctions have deepened their disbelief towards the United States and, in such circumstances, the country will never give up its nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho said at its annual general assembly that North Korea has taken "significant goodwill measures" over the past year, such as halting nuclear and missile tests, dismantling the site of nuclear tests and a promise not to disseminate nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“But we don’t see an adequate response from the U.S.,” he said.
“Without confidence in the U.S., there will be no confidence in our national security, and in such circumstances it is not at all possible to disarm itself unilaterally.”
While Ri reiterated North Korea's well-known complaints about Washington's opposition to a "gradual" approach to denuclearization, according to which North Korea would be rewarded for every successive step, its statements appeared significant in not rejecting unilateral denuclearization just because Pyongyang had gone through this in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first summit between the U.S. president and the North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12, when Kim pledged to work on "denuclearizing the Korean peninsula," while Trump pledged North Korea's security guarantees.
North Korea is seeking a formal end to the 1950–53 war conflict, but the United States has said Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also withstood calls to ease tough international sanctions on North Korea.
“The U.S. insists on ‘denuclearizing the first’ and increasing pressure on sanctions to force its targets, and even objecting to ‘declaring the end of the war’,” Ri said.
"The impression that sanctions can bring us to our knees is the dream of people who don't know anything about us.
But the problem is that ongoing sanctions deepen our distrust.”
Ri made no mention of plans for a second summit between Kim and Trump, which the U.S. leader highlighted at the U.N. earlier this week.
Instead, the minister highlighted three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months, adding: "If South Korea and not the U.S
However, the tone of Ri's speech was dramatically different from that of last year, when he told the U.N
At the U.N. this year, Trump, who threatened the “total destruction” of North Korea last year, praised Kim for his courage to take action to disarmament, but said a lot of work has yet to be done and sanctions must remain in force until North Korea is completely denuclearized.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn't have a time frame for it, he said: "If it takes two years, three years or five months - it doesn't matter."
China and Russia say the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
U.S. Secretary of Foreign Affairs Mike Pompeo told the U.N. Security Council on Thursday: “Enforcing Security Council Sanctions must continue decisively and unceasingly until we become aware of a complete, final, verified denuclearization”
The Security Council unanimously supports sanctions against North Korea since 2006 in an effort to suppress the financing of Pyongyangu's nuclear and ballistic missile programs.
Pompeo and Ri met at the U.N. General Assembly and Pompeo said he would visit Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit.
Pompeo had already visited North Korea three times this year, but his last trip didn't end well.
He left Pyongyang in July, saying progress had been made only for North Korea to declare about him within hours that he had raised "gangster demands."
North Korea has pledged this month to meet with Moon to dismantle the missile compound as well as a nuclear complex if the United States adopts "correspondent measures."
He stated that Kim had notified him that the "appropriate measures" he hopes are the security safeguards that Trump has promised in Singapore and is moving towards normalizing relations with Washington.
Harvard students take part in a course designed to teach them adequate rest
A new course at Harvard University this year prompted all of their students to sleep more, in an effort to combat the growing culture of macho studies throughout the night when students don't sleep and cheer with caffeine.
Academics have found that students at the world's most renowned university are often clueless when it comes to the basics of self-care.
The course was designed by Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School of Medicine and Brigham hospital specialist and women's hospital, who believes to be the first of its kind in the U.S.
He was inspired to start the course after lecturing on the impact of sleep deprivation on learning.
“At the end, a girl came to me and said, ‘Why are you just telling me now, in my last year?’
She said no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep - which surprised me," she told The Telegraph.
The course, which was introduced for the first time this year, explains to students basic information about how good sleep habits help academic and athletic performance and improve their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, a professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and executive director of the university's health services, said the university decided to introduce the course after discovering that the students were really seriously unsleeping during the week.
The hour-long course includes a number of interactive tasks.
In one part there is an image of student dormitories, where students click on cups with coffee, curtains, sneakers and books about the effects of caffeine and light and how athletic performance is affected by lack of sleep and the importance of proper sleeping habits.
In another section, participants are informed about how long-term deprivation of sleep can increase the risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression and cancer.
A map of a campus with interactive icons encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
“We know that the behavior of students will not change immediately.
However, we believe that they have the right to know - just as you have the right to know the effects of smoking cigarettes on health," Professor Czeisler added.
The culture of pride in “studying all night” still exists, adding that modern technology and ever-increasing pressure on students mean that the problem of lack of sleep is also increasing.
Plenty of sleep of good quality should be the “secret weapon” of the student to fight stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he said – even so as there is no weight gain, because sleep deprivation switches the brain to starvation mode, so one is still hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old California student in chemical and physical biology, helped Professor Czeisler design a course that one of his classes graduated last year during his first year at Harvard.
He said the course opened his eyes and inspired him to push for the whole course.
He hopes that the next step will be to ask all postgraduate students to complete a similar degree program before entering a competitive institution.
The professor advised students to consider setting an alarm on the time to go to bed, as well as when to wake up, and be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light” emitted by electronic screens and LED lighting, which can upset your circadian rhythm, leading to problems falling asleep.
Livingston 1 - 0 Rangers: Menga's goal sank Gerrardo's man
Rangers suffered another loss when Dolly Menga's shot cemented Steven Gerrard's dissatisfaction with a 1-0 defeat at Livingstone.
Ibrox has struggled to record its first win from February 4 to February 1 at St Johnstone, but Gary Holt's team has only caused Gerrardo's second defeat in 18 games as a manager to leave his post with eight points from leading Ladbrokes Premiership Hearts leaders.
Menga struck seven minutes before half-time and the Rangers line-up seemed uninspired and a determination to cope.
While the Rangers are now falling to sixth place, Livingston are climbing third and just behind the Hibernian team on a one-wicket difference.
And there could be further Rangers problems after line referee Calum Spence had to be treated with a head injury after an object from the auditorium apparently hit him.
Gerrard made eight changes to his side that swept past Ayr into the Betfred Cup semi-finals.
On the other hand, Holt went with the same Livi 11 who picked up a Hearts point last week and would be delighted as his well-trained team choked on their opponents at every turn.
Rangers may have dominated possession, but Livingston has done more with the ball on their side.
They should have scored just two minutes after the start when Menga first dropped Scott Pittman on Allan McGregor's goal, but the midfielder buried his great chance.
Keaghan Jacobs then found captain Craig Halkett, but his defensive partner Alan Lithgow could only have tried at the back post.
Rangers have taken control, but there seemed to be more hope in the final third than beliefs.
Alfredo Morelos certainly felt he should have a penalty for a quarter-hour lead when they collided with Steven Lawless, but referee Steven Thomson swept Colombia's challenge off the table.
Rangers managed just two shots on the finish in the first half, but former Ibrox goalie Liam Kelly was surprised by Lassana Coulibala's header and Ovie Ejaria's tame shot.
While Livi's opener after 34 minutes could have been against the run of the game, no one can deny that they deserved it already for the toil.
Rangers once again failed to deal with a deep Jacobs set.
Scott Arfield didn't respond when Declan Gallagher sent the ball to Scott Robinson, who was calm and took Menga to an easy finish.
Gerrard took action during the break, swapping Coulibaly for Ryan Kent, and the exchange had an almost immediate result when the wing plunged into Morelosa, but the formidable Kelly struggled from the line and blocked the attack.
But Livingston continued to draw visitors into the game of exactly the kind they like when Lithgow and Halkett metal one long ball after another.
Holt's side may have deepened its lead in the final stages, but McGregor stood up well to block Jacobs before Litgow ran from a corner.
Rangers substitute Glenn Middleton had another delayed claim of punishment when he became entangled with Jacobs, but Thomson averted again.
Almanach: Inventor of Geiger's counter
And now the page from our almanac “Sunday morning”: September 30, 1882, today it is 136 years old, and it is still increasing... the day when the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity, an invention that eventually led to a device known as Geiger's counter.
Geiger Counter became a constant of pop culture at the time, as in the 1950 film “Bells of Coronado”, starring Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
Man: “What on earth is this?”
Rogers: "It's Geiger's counter that's used to locate radioactive minerals like uranium.
When you put these headphones on, you can really hear the effects of atoms shot by radioactivity in minerals.
Evans: “Okay, it’s cracking right now!”
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
But the invention that bears his name lives on.
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to “see” dangerous cells
A new cancer vaccine can teach the immune system to "see" dangerous cells and kill them
Vaccine teaches the immune system to recognize dangerous cells as part of treatment
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from the patient and their transformation in the laboratory
Then they can “see” a protein that is common to many cancers, and then injected again
The trial vaccine shows promising results in patients with multiple cancers.
One woman treated with the vaccine, which teaches the immune system to recognize dangerous cells, has been experiencing ovarian cancer for more than 18 months.
The method involves the extraction of immune cells from the patient, their transformation into a laboratory, so they can “see” a protein common in many cancers called HER2, and then be reapplied to the cell.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute in Bethesda, Maryland said: "Our results suggest that we have developed a very promising vaccine."
HER2 “directs the growth of several types of cancer,” including breast, ovary, lung and colorectal cancer cancer, Professor Berzofsky explained.
A similar approach, when immune cells were collected from patients and "taught" to focus on cancer cells, has already been promoted in the treatment of the type of leukemia.
Kanye West took part in a pro-Trump sermon, in his maga cap, after his appearance on SNL.
It didn't go very well
Kanye West was booed at the Saturday Night Live studio after a bewilded appearance during which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump and said he would run in 2020.
After singing a third song that night called Ghost Town, in which he was wearing a Make America Great Again hat, he began spitting at Democrats and reiterating his endorsement of President Trump.
“I often talk to white people and they say, ‘How can you like Trump, he’s a racist?”
“If I didn’t care about racism, I wouldn’t have been in America for a long time,” he said.
SNL launched a parody with Matte Damon starring the Hollywood starring to make fun of Brett Kavanaugh's testimony before the Senate Judiciary Judiciary Committee about allegations to rape Christine Blasey Ford.
Although it wasn't broadcast, footage of West's theatrical rhetorication was uploaded to social media by comedian Chris Rock.
It's unclear whether Rock was trying to mock West.
West also complained to spectators that he got his adornment of his head nicely even behind the scenes.
"I was bullied behind the scenes.
They said, "Don't get there with the hat."
It's bullying!
They also said I was in the wrong place,” Examiner said.
West continued: "Do you want to see the wrong place?" and added that he would "put on his superman suit because it means you can't say what to do." Do you want the world to move forward?
Treat love.”
