HIV/Aids: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China reported a 14% increase in the number of its citizens living with HIV and AIDS.
According to official figures, more than 820 000 people are affected by this virus, according to official figures.
Approximately 40 000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
The vast majority of new cases were transmitted by sex, which marked a change from the past.
Traditionally, HIV spreads through some parts of China as a result of infected blood transfusions.
But the number of people infected with HIV in this way has been reduced to almost zero, Chinese health officials said at a conference in Yunnan province.
The number of people living with HIV and AIDS in China increased by 100 000 people year-on-year.
Transmitting HIV through sex is an acute problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against LGBT people is reportedly still widespread.
Due to the country's conservative values, studies estimate that 70-90% of men who have sex with men will eventually marry women.
Many of the transmissions of diseases come from the lack of sexual protection during these intercourses.
Since 2003, the Chinese government has pledged a universal approach to the treatment of HIV as part of an effort to address the problem.
