Conservative sites like Gateway Pundit and RedState have announced that the IP address, which sources of contributions, is affiliated with the Waters office and has posted information about Waters staff members, Hill says.
"This unfounded assertion is a completely untrue and absolute lie," Waters continued.
"A member of my staff - whose identities, personal information and security have been jeopardised as a result of these fraudulent and false allegations - were in no way responsible for leaking such information.
This unfounded statement is a completely untrue and absolute lie.”
Waters's statement quickly earned online criticism, including that of former White House press secretary Ari Fleischer.
"This denial is angry," Fleischer wrote.
“This suggests he doesn’t have the temperament to be a member of Congress.”
When someone is accused of something they didn't do, they can't be angry.
He must not be defiant.
He must not question the plaintiff's motives.
It has to be calm and not get excited.”
Fleischer seemed to compare Waters's reaction with criticism of Democrats towards Judge Kavanaugho, whom they criticized because he seemed too angry during Thursday's hearing.
Omar Navarro, the Republican nominee who took part in the election against Waters, also expressed his thoughts on Twitter.
