What is the Global Citizen Festival and what has it done to reduce poverty?
On this Saturday, New York City's unival music event Global Citizen Festival, which offers a very impressive array of stars and an equally impressive mission for the end of world poverty.
In the 7th edition, Global Citizen will welcome tens of thousands of people who will meet in Central Park to enjoy not only such artists as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the real goal of the event, which is the end of extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, launched in 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty project, an international group to support ending poverty by increasing the number of people who are actively fighting against it.
In order to get a free ticket to the event (if you weren’t willing to pay for a VIP ticket), visitors had to complete a series of assignments or “actions,” such as volunteering, an email to a world leader, a phone call, or other meaningful ways to help raise awareness of their goal of ending poverty.
But how successful has Global Citizen been now 12 years before it achieves its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert really in a way to persuade them to demand an action, or is it just another case of so-called “clictivism” – people who feel that by signing an online petition or sending a tweet will make a difference?
The Global Citizen says it has recorded more than 19 million "actions" by its supporters since 2011, trying to achieve different goals.
He says these actions have contributed to world leaders announcing commitments and measures of more than US$37 billion to affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group announced 390 commitments and notifications resulting from its activities, of which at least $10bn has already been paid or funded.
The group estimates that hedged funds still have a direct impact on nearly 649 million people worldwide.
Key commitments include the Power of Nutrition project, a British partnership of investors and implementers who have pledged to “help children grow to their full potential,” promising Rwananda to provide 35 million dollars to help end malnutrition in the county after receiving more than 4,700 tweets from Global Citizens.
"With the support of the British government, donors, national governments and Global Citizens like you, we can only make a note in history out of social injustice in the form of malnutrition," Tracey Ullman, ambassador of the Power of Nutrition project, told the crowd during a live concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that after more than £5,000 actions calling on the U.K. to improve nutrition for mothers and children, the government announced funding for the Power of Nutrition project, which will appeal to 5 million women and children with nutritional interventions.
In response to one of the most common questions on your website asking, “why do you think we can end extreme poverty?”
“It’s going to be a long and hard journey – sometimes we fall and fail.
But just like the great civil rights and anti-apartheid movement before us, we will succeed because we are stronger together.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are among the performers at this year's New York edition to host Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
