A week after an official Chinese newspaper published a four-page ad in a U.S. newspaper dedicated to the mutual benefits of U.S.-China trade, a U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump mentioned last Wednesday's China Daily surcharge in the Des Moines - the status of Iowa's largest sales newspaper - after accusing China of trying to mix into the U.S. congressional elections in the U.S. on November 6.
Trump's allegations that Beijing was attempting to meddle in the U.S. election called what U.S. officials said was a new phase of Washington's escalating campaign to put pressure on China.
While it is customary for foreign governments to advertise in support of trade, Beijing and Washington are currently stuck in an escalating trade war manifested at the level of mutual imports.
Chinese retaliatory sanctions at the start of a trade war were designed to hit exporters in states such as Iowa, which supported Trump's Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, the U.S. ambassador to China, and former long-time governor of Iowa, a major exporter of agricultural goods to China, said Beijing had injured U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
Branstad wrote in an opinion in Sunday's Des Moines Register that China is now doubling this bullying by publishing propaganda ads in our own free press.""
“In distributing its propaganda, the Chinese government uses the U.S. Valuable Tradition of Free Speech and the Free Press by placing paid advertising in the Des Moines Register.”
“In contrast, in a newspaper stand on the street in Beijing, you will find limited dissenting voices and you will not see any real reflection of disparate views that the Chinese might have on China’s disturbing economic career, given that the media belongs to ‘The Chinese Communist Party’,” he wrote.
