Inventor of World Wide Web plans to start new internet and overtake Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, launches a startup that is trying to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest technology legend project, Inrupt, is a company that builds on Berners-Lee's open source platform called Solid.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and which people have access to information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the goal of the Inrupt project was "world domination."
“We have to do it now,” he said.
"It's a historical moment."
The application uses Solid technology, which allows people to create their own “personal online data storage” or POD.
It may include contact lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library, and other personal and professional instruments.
It’s like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify are available in one browser and all at the same time.
What’s special about personal online data storage is that it’s entirely up to the user to determine who’s going to have access to what kind of information.
The company calls it “personal empowerment through data.”
According to CEO John Bruce, the purpose of the Inrupt project is to bring resources, processes, and appropriate skills to help the Solid platform be available to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform bought by IBM, some employees who work on the project have collaborated on a project and a community of volunteer programmers.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world can create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said that he and his team with "Facebook and Google don't talk about making a complete change where all their business models are flipped upside down overnight.
“We don’t ask for their permission.”
Berners-Lee wrote in his post on media published on Saturday that the mission of the Inrupt project is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that will help protect the integrity and quality of a new site built on the Solid platform.
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in discussion of net neutrality.
Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm incredibly optimistic about this next era of the site," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Celebration of a clergy who received Victoria's Cross during World War 1
There have been hundreds of years since the only clergy of the Anglican Church acquired Victoria's Cross as a warrior during the First World War.
Lieutenant Reverend Bernard Vann was awarded on September 29, 1918 in an attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
Four days later, however, he was killed by a sniper, and he had never known that he had won Britain's highest military award.
His two grandchildren unveiled a memorial stone for him on Saturday during a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire.
One of his great-granddaughters, Michael Vann, said it was "brilliantly symbolic" that the stone had been unveiled exactly 100 years after his grandfather's victorious achievement.
According to London Gazette on September 29, 1918, Lieutenant Colonel Lt Col Vann led his battalion through the Canal de Saint-Quentin with a very dense fog and heavy gunfire from field cannons and machine guns."
Later, he got to the front line and, with "the utmost courage," led the line forward before he plunged independently into the firing field and knocked out three separated units.
Lieutenant Colonel Vanna was killed by a German sniper on October 4, 1918 – just a month before the end of the war.
72-year-old Michael Vann said his grandfather's actions were "something I could never do, but at the same time something that's humiliating."
Together with his brother, Dr. James Vann laid a wreath after the parade, led by Brentwoord Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said he "felt a great honor of being able to participate in the parade," adding that "the valousness of a true hero is manifested by the support provided by a lot of people."
MMA fans stayed all night to watch Bellator 206, instead getting Peppa's pig
Imagine, you've been waiting all night to watch Bellatore 206, only to be denied a major event.
The receipt from San José contained 13 points, including six on the main card and a live broadcast took place in the UK all night on Channel 5.
At 6 o'clock in the morning, when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing for a mutual match, viewers in the UK remained amazed as the news turned into a show about Peppa's piggy bank.
Some remained uneashy after waking until the early hours of the morning because of the match.
On Twitter, one of the fans described a change in broadcasts to a children's show as "a certain form of bad joke."
"According to a government regulation, such content is not appropriate at 6 a.m., and it is therefore necessary to broadcast children's shows," said Dave Schwartz, Bellator's senior vice president of marketing and communications after being asked about the broadcast.
“Peppa Pig”, yes.”
Bellator president Scott Coker has said they will work on the programme so that viewers in the U.K. will also be involved in the UK in the future.
"I think when I'm thinking about broadcasting, we can probably make it," Cooker said.
"But it's six o'clock in the morning on Sunday and we won't make it until the Sunday of our time, Monday of their time.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, when the program changed, a lot of texts went back and forth, and not all of them were exactly friendly.
We tried to fix it, we thought it was a technical issue.
But it wasn't, it was the government.
I promise you it won't happen next time.
We're going to reduce it to five games instead of six - as we normally do - and we've tried with all our strength to keep the promise given to the fans and we didn't succeed.
It's an unfortunate situation.'
Desert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt "inferious" because of sexuality
Tom Daley, an Olympic water jumper, says he grew up feeling inferiority towards everyone because of his sexuality – but that motivated him to pursue success.
The 24-year-old athlete said he didn't realize that "not everyone is like him."
On Radio 4 Desert Island Disc, introduced by Lauren Laverne, he said he spoke out loud about the rights of gays to give others "hopes."
He also said that, thanks to his ability to become a parent, he was less interested in winning the Olympics.
A regular presenter of the long-term Kirsty Young show had several months off because of illness.
Daley, who appeared as a castaway in Lavern’s first program, said he felt “inferiorable” than anyone else who grew up at the same time with him because “it wasn’t socially unacceptable for you to like boys and girls.”
“Until now, these feelings of inferiority and otherness have been real things that have given me the strength and power to succeed.”
He wanted to prove that he was “someone,” he said not to disappoint anyone who could possibly find out how it was with his sexuality.
The two-time bronze Olympic medal winner has become a high-profile LGBT rights campaigner and used his presence at this year's Commonwealth Games in Australia to appeal to multiple countries to decriminalise homosexuality.
He said he spoke out because he was lucky enough to be able to live openly without consequences and wanted to give others "hopes."
This three-time world champion stated that falling in love with a man – American film director Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013 – “fell unprepared.”
Daley married the Oskar holder, who is 20 years older, last year, but said the age difference was never a problem.
“When you experience so much at such a young age – he was at his first Olympiad when he was 14 years old and his father died of cancer three years later – he said it wasn’t easy to find someone of the same age who had experienced similar rises and downs.
In June, the pair adopted son Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley said his entire point of view had "changed."
“If you’d asked me last year, it’d all be in the style of ‘I want to win a gold medal,’” he said.
"You know, there's bigger stuff than Olympic gold medals.
My Olympic medal is Robbie.”
His son was named after his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 after being diagnosed with brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn't come to terms with dying, and the last thing he's asked was if they already have tickets for the 2012 London Olympics because he wanted to be in the front rows.
"I couldn't tell him - you won't be in the first place, Dad," he said.
"I held his hand when he stopped breathing and it wasn't until the moment he stopped breathing and he was dead that I finally realised he wasn't invincible," he said.
A year later, Daley took part in the 2012 Olympics and won bronze.
“I’ve known I’ve dreamed about it all my life – jumping in front of our homers at the Olympic Games, I don’t know a better feeling.”
It also inspired the selection of his first song – Proud by Heather Small – which resonated with him at the opening of the Olympics and still evoked goose bumps in it.
Desert Island Discs airs on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 a.m. BST.
Mickelson, who was out of form, ended up on the bench at the Ryder Cup on Saturday
American Phil Mickelson would have achieved a record on Sunday when he played a 47-game Ryder Cup game, but he'd have to fine-tune the form to prevent that it was an unfortunate milestone.
Mickelson, who played at events held every twelftie, was sent by captain Jim Furyk during Saturday's four-balls and foursomes to the bench.
Instead of being at the center of events, as has often been the case in the U.S., the five-time main winner spent his day alternately between cheerleaders and working on the game in a row that he hoped to fix what troubled him.
This 48-year-old has never been the most direct of drivers, even at the peak of his career, so he's not the perfect choice for a narrow Le Golf National course, where a long rugged terrain routinely punish stray shots.
And if the golf course itself isn't disturbing enough, Mickelson is facing an accurate British Open champion Francescu Molinari in his ninth-sunday match, who has won all four of his matches this week in a team with rookie Tommy Fleetwood.
If the Americans, who had four fewer points, had launched 12 separate swords, they had escaped a quick start, Mickelson's sword could have proved absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in his husband and added nothing else.
"He completely understands the role he's had today, patted me on the back, put his arm around my shoulders, and said he'd be ready tomorrow," Furyk said.
“He’s so confident.
He's a member of the Straw Hall, and he's been so beneficial to these teams in the past and this week.
I guess I couldn't have imagined he's going to play two swords.
I've imagined more, but that's how it's evolved and we thought we had to go down that path.
He wants to participate, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson will surpass Nick Falde's record in most of the Ryder Cup west played Sunday.
It could mean the end of his career in the Ryder Cup, which has never reached the heights of his individual record.
Mickelson scored 18 wins, 20 losses and seven draws, although Furyk says his team presence has yielded some intangible results.
"He's funny, he's sarcastic, he's funny, he likes to make fun of people and he's a great companion in the team room," he explained.
"I think the younger players also had fun when they went to see him, this week, which was fun to watch.
It gives a lot more than just a game.”
European captain Thomas Bjorn knows the big lead may soon disappear
Thomas Bjorn, the European captain, knows from experience that a big lead heading into singles from recent days in the Ryder Cup can easily turn into an inconvenient ride.
Dane made his debut at the 1997 match in Valderrama, where a squad led by captain Seve Ballesteros had a five-point lead over the Americans, but reached the finish line with an all-time 141⁄2-131⁄2 victory.
"You have to keep reminding yourself that we had a big lead in Valderrama, as well as in Brooklyn, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but only closely," Bjorn said, in the picture, after watching when they won 5-3 on both Friday and yesterday, for Le Golf National to lead 10-6.
So history and every member of that team have shown me it's not over.
You'll be completely bored tomorrow.
Go there and do all the right things.
It's not behind you until all those points are on the board.
We've got a goal and that's to try and we're focusing on that.
I've said I'll focus on 12 players on our side, but we also know what to stand up on the other side - the best players in the world."
Bjorn, delighted at the way his players fared on a complex golf course, added: "I could never do it myself.
There'll be another animal tomorrow.
There are individual performances prepared tomorrow and that's very difficult.
It's great to be out with your partner when it's going well, but when you're supposed to be there for yourself, your golf player's capacity is fully verified.
That's the message you have to convey to the players, get the best out of yourself tomorrow.
Now you're leaving your partner behind and he's got to go and get the best of himself.
Unlike Bjorn, opponent Jim Furyk will make sure that his players play better as individuals than as partners. The exceptions are Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have scored three points out of four.
Furyk was personally on both sides of these great twists when he was part of the winning team in Brooklino to end up as a loser when Europe achieved "The Miracle in Medinah."
“I remember every damn word,” he answered the question of how Ben Crenshaw, the 1999 captain, encouraged his players to head into the final day.
"We've got 12 important games tomorrow, but you'd like to start quickly as you did in Brooklyn, like in Medinah.
When this pace goes in one direction, it puts a lot of pressure on those middle matches.
That's how we've set the line-up and ensured that the boys feel like you're knowing like we're supposed to do magic tomorrow.'
Thomas was tasked with trying to lead the counter-attack and facing Rory McIlroy in a top-notch match with players: Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter, which are other Europeans in the top half of the standings.
"I went with that group of boys in that order because I think he's covering all the way," Bjorn said of his choice of singles.
Germany's new warship postponed again
Germany's newest naval frigate was recognized in 2014 to replace aging warships from the Cold War era, but it won't be available for at least one more year because of faulty systems and the snowy coast, according to local media.
The commissioning of the "Rheinland-Pfalz", an all-new Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigate, has now been postponed to the first half of 2019, according to Die Zeit.
The vessel was supposed to become part of the navy in 2014, but the demanding problems after delivery made the fate of this ambitious project uncomfortable.
Four Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels ordered by the navy in 2007 will serve as a replacement for aging Bremen-class frigates.
It is clear that they will be equipped with a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft and anti-ship ballistic missiles and certain hidden technologies, such as reduced radar and infrared and acoustic elements.
Other important features include a long maintenance period – it should be possible to deploy the newest frigates for up to two years outside domestic ports.
However, constant delays mean that cutting-edge naval ships – which were supposed to allow Germany to send forces overseas – will be obsolete until commissioning, according to Die Zeit.
The infamous F125 frigate made it to the newspaper last year, when the German navy officially refused to put the vessel into operation and returned it to Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
This was the first time the navy had returned the ship to the manufacturer after handover.
Few were known about the reasons for the return, but the German media quoted a number of fundamental “software bugs and defects in the metal elements” of defects, due to which the warship would be unusable when deployed into combat.
The flaws in the software were particularly significant because the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessel will be run by the crew of some 120 sailors – half of the workers who were needed on Bremen-class frigates.
It has also been showned to have considerably excessive weight, reducing its performance and limiting the Navy's ability to add future innovation.
It is believed that the 7,000-ton ship Rheinland-Pfal will be twice as heavy as ships of a similar class used by the Germans during World War II.
In addition to defects in metal elements, the price of the whole project is also a problem – including crew training.
It is said that out of the original 2.2 billion euros, the price has climbed to a staggering 3.1 billion euros.
Problems with the latest frigates have become important especially in the light of recent warnings that Germany's naval power is declining.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, head of the German parliament's defense committee, confirmed that the naval army was indeed "comitting ships capable of deployment."
This official said the problems had been rolled in continuously because the old ships had been decommissioned, but no replacements had been provided.
He lamented that none of the Baden-Wuerttemberg-class frigates were capable of becoming part of the navy's gear.
The National Trust is listening to the secret life of bats
New research is underway to uncover how bats use the landscape to hunt food.
There is a hope that research findings will shed new light on the behavior of unique flying mammals and help manage future activities to preserve this species.
The study, carried out by scientists of the National Trust for Scotland association, will track the bats whistling and the smallest as well as brown long and aquatic at the Inverewe Gardens botanic garden in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed in key areas in this bat housing estate, which will monitor the activities of bats throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using manual detectors.
The professional audio analysis of all recordings determines the frequency of bat calls and what individual species do.
Subsequently, a habitat map will be released and a report that will create a detailed landscape image of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, natural resource conservation adviser for NTS, hopes that the results will show which areas of habitat are most important to bats, and how individual species use them.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management work, such as the formation of meadows, and the best practice in maintaining forested areas for bats and other related species.
Bat populations in Scotland and across the United Kingdom have declined to a large extent in the last century.
They are threatened as a result of construction and territorial development that affect colonies and the natural environment.
Wind turbines and lighting, as well as traps for flies and the chemical treatment of building materials, as well as cat attacks, can also pose a risk.
Bats aren't really blind.
However, because they hunt at night, their ears are more useful than their eyes when hunting prey.
They use an ingenious echo-location technique to track bugs and obstacles in flight.
NTS, responsible for caring for more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land in the country, is taking bats very seriously.
It has trained experts who regularly conduct surveys, colony inspections, and sometimes rescue efforts.
This organization has even set up the first and only dedicated bat reserve at the Threave housing estate in Dumfries and Galloway, home to eight out of ten species of bats in Scotland.
Housing estate manager David Thompson says it's an ideal territory for them.
"Here in Threave, we've got a great area for bats," he said.
“We have old buildings, lots of ancient trees and the best habitat.
But there's still too much we don't know about bats, so the work we're doing here and in other places will help us better understand what they need to survive."
It emphasizes the importance of controlling bats before starting maintenance work in real estate, because it is possible that the unconscious destruction of a single maternity nesting site can kill up to 400 females and cubs, which can destroy the entire local population.
Bats are protected and it's illegal to kill them, harasse, disturb or destroy their colonies.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish representative of the Bat Conservation Trust, encourages the public to put her hand to work and help.
She said: "We still have a lot to learn about our bats and a lot of species that we don't know how their populations are doing."
Ronaldo denies rallies and his lawyers are preparing to sue a German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo called the rape allegations "false news" and said people "wanted to make themselves visible" by using his name.
His lawyers are preparing to sue the German daily Der Spiegel, which reported on these allegations.
This Portuguese and attacker for Juventus has been accused of raping American Kathryn Mayorga in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
He reportedly paid her US$375 000 to keep quiet about the case, Der Spiegel reported on Friday.
A few hours since the publication of information about the allegations, 33-year-old Ronaldo spoke in a live Instagram video to his 142-million-dollar followers, calling the information "false news."
"No, no, no, no, no.
What they said today is fake news," the five-time Golden Ball award winner said.
“They want to make a living in my name.
It's normal.
They want to be famous when they're saying my name, but that's part of their job.
I'm happy and I'm fine," said the player with a smile.
According to Reuters, Rolando's lawyers are preparing to indict Der Spiegel on unfounded allegations, calling it an "inadmissiblent announcement of suspicion in a private area."
Lawyer Christian Schertz said the player will demand compensation for "moral damage equivalent to the seriousness of the breach, which is likely one of the most serious violations of personal rights recently."
The alleged incident was scheduled to take place in June 2009 at the Palms Hotel suite and in a casino in Las Vegas.
After getting acquainted at a nightclub, Ronando and Mayorga reportedly returned to the arcade room, where she allegedly, according to documentation filed in court at the Clark County District Court in Nevada, allegedly annally raped her.
Mayorga claims Ronaldo knelt after the alleged incident, telling her that he was at "99 per cent" a "poster" and "one percent" disappointed.
The documents claim that Ronaldo confirmed that he paid for the sex, but that he was consensual.
Mayorga also declares that she went to the police and had photographs of her injuries taken at the hospital, but later agreed to an out-of-court settlement because she was "fearing retaliation" and feared "public humiliation."
The 34-year-old woman says she is now seeking to resume proceedings because she is still traumatized by the alleged incident.
At the time of allegations of an offence since his transfer to Club Real Madid from Manchester United and this summer he was bought for 100 million euros by the famous Italian club Juve.
Brexit: The U.K. will forever regret the loss of automakers
The UK will "regret it forever" if it loses its status as a world leader in car manufacturing after Brexit, the company's secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that it was "disturbing" that Toyota UK had told the BBC that if Britain had left the EU without a deal, it would have temporarily halted production at Burnaston's plant, near Derby.
"A deal is necessary, "Clark said.
The Japanese automaker said the impact of delays at the border in the event of a Brexit deal could cost jobs.
At a plant in Burnaston where Auris and Avensis cars of the Toyota brand are produced – last year they produced nearly 150 000 cars, 90% of which were exported to other European Union countries.
"My view is that if Britain suddenly left the EU at the end of March, we would see a halt to production at our factory," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's chief executive at Burnaston.
Other automakers, including automakers Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover in the UK, are spreading fears of leaving the EU without a deal when it comes to cross-border trading.
BMW, for example, says it will close its Mini car manufacturing plant in Oxford just a month after Brexit.
The main concerns are related to what manufacturers describe as risks to the supply chain in the event of a no-deal Brexit.
Toyota's production line is in operation "on time", while parts from suppliers in both the UK and the EU are delivered for cars made to order within 37 minutes.
If the UK were to leave the EU without a deal on March 29, there could be a disruption at the border, which, according to the industry, could lead to delays and a shortage of spare parts.
It would be impossible for Toyota to have more than one day in stock at Derbyshire plant, the company said, and thus production would be halted.
Mr Clark said Theresa May's plans to revive future relations with the EU are "accurately calibrated so as not to avoid these border controls."
"We need a deal. We want to have the best trade, which will allow, as I say, not only current success, but also grasp this opportunity," he told BBC Radio 4's Today.
“The evidence not only from Toyota, but also from other manufacturers, is that we need to be absolutely able to continue what is a highly successful set of supply chains.”
Toyota has not been able to say how long production will be halted, but warns in the long run that the added costs would reduce the competitiveness of the plant and ultimately be at the expense of jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has worked at Burnaston for 24 years and is the union's chairman at the plant, said union members were becoming increasingly concerned: "I have experience that once these venues disappear, they will never return.
A government spokesperson said: "We have deployed a precise and reliable plan for our future relations with the EU."
Trump meeting with Rosenstein may be delayed again, according to the White House
Donald Trump's high-level meeting with acting attorney General Rod Rosenstein could be "relegated for another week" as the fight for the post of supreme judge with Brett Kavanaugh continues, the White House said Sunday.
Rosenstein is overseeing the work of Robert Mueller's special council, which examines crackdowns on Russian elections, ties between Trump's aides and Russia and possible judicial obstruction by the president.
Whether Trump releases the acting general's envoy and thus jeopardizes Mueller's independence, Washington's gossip has been full for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein discussed wearing a camera to record conversations with Trump and the possibility of dethroning the president through Amendment 25.
Rosenstein denied the report.
Last Monday, however, he arrived at the White House surrounded by news that he was about to resign.
Instead, a meeting was announced for Thursday with Trump, who was at the headquarters of the United Nations in New York at the time.
Trump said he would "prefer" Rosensten not to be dismissed, but then the meeting was delayed in order not to collide with hearings at the Senate Judicial Committee, where Kavanaugh and one of the women who accused him of sexual harassment, Dr Christine Blasey Ford.
On Friday, Trump ordered the FBI to conduct a week-long investigation into the allegations against Kavanaugh, delaying the Senate's full vote again.
Trump's spokeswoman, Sarah Sanders, appeared in the news on the Fox channel on Sunday.
When asked for a meeting with Rosenstein, she replied: "There hasn't set a date yet, it could be this week, it might be necessary to move for another week with regard to everything else that's going on at the Supreme Court.
But we'll see and I'll always communicate up-to-date information to
Some reporters disagree with this statement: Sanders has not held a press conference with the White House since September 10.
Hoster Chris Wallace asked why.
Sanders said too few press conferences were caused by an aversion to the "theatre" of television reporters, although she said: "I don't agree with the fact that he will perform."
Subsequently, she suggested that the number of direct contacts between Trump and the press would increase.
"The president is holding more sessions with questions and answers than any president in front of him," she added, without giving evidence. "We've been looking at those numbers."
The conferences will continue to be held, Sanders said, but "if press officials had the opportunity to ask the president of the United States directly, it would undoubtedly be better than talking to me.
We're trying, and you've seen we've been doing this in the last couple of weeks, and there's going to be press conferences where you can talk to the president of the United States."
Trump regularly accepts inquiries when he leaves the White House or attends open sessions or press conferences with visiting senior officials.
Separate press conferences are rare.
This week in New York, the president probably showed why, with his vacant and sometimes bizarre appearance in front of gathered reporters.
The health secretary writes to EU workers at NHS Scotland not to be afraid of Brexit
The Minister of Health has written to EU employees who work at Scotland's NHS to express their gratitude and wishes for them to stay even after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman MSP sent a letter less than six months before Britain's departure from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already pledged to settle the cost of applications for the settlement status of EU citizens working in transferred public services.
Freeman wrote in her letter: "Over the summer, negotiations between the UK and the EU on distance are ongoing, and are moving towards expected decisions this autumn.
However, the British government is also taking steps in preparations for a possible no-deal gap scenario.
I know it must be a very disturbing time for all of you.
That's which I'd like to reassure you how much I appreciate the contribution of every member of staff, regardless of nationality.
Colleagues from all over the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of health services and are beneficial for patients and communities to whom we provide services.
Scotland is absolutely your home, and our fervent wish is for you to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie advances emergency surgery after head injuries
Ejected Tigers defender Christion Abercrombie advanced emergency surgery after he sustained head injuries in Saturday's 31-27 defeat from rival Vanderbilt Commodores, said Mike Organ of Tennessee.
Tennessee head coach Rod Reed told reporters that the injury occurred shortly before half-time.
"He came to the sideline and just collapsed there," Reed said.
Coaches and paramedics gave Abercrombie oxygen on the sideline and then put him on a stretcher and prepared him for further assessment.
A Tennessee official told Chrisz Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee that Abercrombie progressed surgery at Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris added that there were "no details on the type/range of the injuries" yet, and that state Tennessee was trying to find out when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a sophomore in a red T-shirt, undergoes his first season for the state of Tennessee after being transferred from Illinois.
He had five total saves prior to finishing the game on Saturday, which means a total of 18 saves in the season.
Foreign buyers will be charged a higher property tax when buying real estate in the UK
A foreign buyer will be charged a higher property tax when they buy property in the UK, with extra money being used to help the homeless under the new Tory plans
This act will neutralize the success of Corbyn's move, which aims to attract young voters
Increasing the obligation of a higher property tax will apply to people who don't pay taxes in the UK
It is expected to increase the Treasury by £120 million a year – to help homeless people
Buyers from abroad will pay a higher tax on the purchase of real estate when they purchase real estate in the UK, with extra money being used to help homeless people, Theresa May announces today.
The act will be seen as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn's campaign to attract young voters with promises to provide more affordable accommodation and a focus on high-income people.
Increasing the property purchase tax will affect individuals and firms who do not pay taxes in the UK, while the money will also strengthen the government's efforts to struggle to sleep without a roof over their heads.
The surcharge, which is an addition to the current obligation to pay property purchase tax, including the higher levels introduced two years ago for second homes and purchases for rental purposes, can reach up to three percent.
It is projected to increase funds by up to £120 million a year in the Treasury.
Estimates say 13 per cent of citizens who are not residents of the U.K. are buoying out prices and making it difficult for those who buy for the first time to get on the rankings.
Many rich areas of the country – especially in the capital – have become “ghost cities” precisely because of the high number of buyers from abroad who spend most of their time outside the country.
New principles emerged just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for a reduction in property purchase tax to help more young people at their own first house.
Accusing large construction firms of keeping property prices high by occupying land but not taking advantage of it, urged Ms. May to withdraw quotas from affordable homes to correct Britain's "adversity of homes."
Mr Corbyn has announced an eye-catching series of proposed housing reforms, including the management of rents and the termination of "safe" evictions.
He also wants to increase the powers of committees to build new homes.
Ms May said: "Last year I said I wanted to devote my efforts as prime minister to restoring the British dream - so that every new generation would have a better life.
And that means fixing our damaged property market.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and shape their lives.
However, it cannot be right for this to be easy for entities that do not live in Britain and for foreign companies, so that they can buy houses just like hard-working UK residents.
For a lot of people, the dream of their own home is too distant, and the disgrace of sleeping without a roof over their heads is too real.”
Jack Ross: ‘My highest ambition is to drive Scotland’
Sunderland boss Jack Ross says his "highest ambition" is to become Scotland's manager at some stage.
The 42-year-old Scot is delighted with the challenge of restoring the north-eastern club, which currently holds its third position in the Premier League, just three degrees below the top.
He switched to Stadium of Light this summer after leading St Mirren back the Scottish Premiership leagues last season.
“I wanted to play for my country like a player.
I got rank B and it was," Ross told BBC channel Scotland's Sportsound.
"But I grew up watching Scotia in Hampden with my dad when I was a kid, and that's always something that pulls me back.
But such an opportunity would only come if I was successful in the management of the club.”
Rosson's forerunners as Sunderland's managers were: Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic boss says he felt no anxiety about the succession of such established names at such a big club, having previously rejected overtures from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
"Success for me at that time would be a question of whether I can return this club to the Premier League.
Because of the structure and equipment at this club, it undoubtedly belongs to the Premier League," he said.
“It’s not an easy task to put him back there, but I’d probably only feel successful if I was able to return the club there.”
Ross has been in charge for only three years, having previously served as assistant boss at Dumarton and spent 15 months among Hearts coaches.
He then helped the Alloa club to recover from their descent into the third group and transformed St Mirren from an initiative decline to the winner of the championship title in the following season.
And Ross says he's got a much better feeling right now than he's ever haven players at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical at any time in his career.
"They were probably real intersections," he recalls of the time Alloa took over.
“I really believed that leadership was right for me, more than playing.
It sounds weird because I was good, earning well and enjoying reasonable heights.
But playing can be challenging.
There's a lot of things you have to go through every week.
I'm still going through them in terms of the stresses and the pressure of the work, but I just have a good feeling in charge.
I've always wanted to be a manager and now I'm doing it, I feel like I'm feeling myself the most in my whole adult life right now.'
You can listen to the full interview on Sportsound on Sunday, September 30 on the Radio Scotland channel from 12:00 to 13:00 BST
The research found that a great time on the pint is Saturday at 17:30 p.m
The summer heatwave has boosted the revenues of Britain's pubs struggling, but increased the pressure on restaurant chains.
Figures show that groups of pubs and bars saw sales up 2.7 per cent in July - but restaurant revenue was down 4.8 percent.
Peter Martin of the consulting firm CGA, which produced the final reports, said: "Stable sunshine and longer-than-expected England's participation in the World Cup meant that July was followed by a similar pattern as previous June, when pub attendances increased by 2.8 per cent, but restaurants were even more affected.
The decline in restaurant sales by 1.8 percent in June deteriorated even further in July.
Pubs and bars appealing for drinks have so fared in the strongest the same way that the restaurants were offended.
Pubs, whose main sales are from food, have also suffered through the sun, though not as dramatically as restaurant operators.
Looks like people just wanted to go out for a drink.
Between controlled values and bars, beverage sales rose 6.6 per cent for the month, while food sales fell by three percent."
Paul Newman, from an analytics firm of leisure activities and hospitality, said: "These results are a continuation of a trend that has been going on since the end of April.
