Earthquakes and tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have died in a powerful earthquake and tsunami that struck the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday. According to authorities, the death toll is expected to rise further.
As communications are out of order, auxiliary units could not obtain any information from Donggala, an area north of Palu, which is closer to the epicenter of a 7.5-degree earthquake.
More than 16,000 people were evacuated from Palo after the outbreak.
Here are some key facts about the city of Palu and Donggala Regency, on Sulawesi Island:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulawesi province, located at the end of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, and its population was estimated at 379,800 in 2017.
The city was celebrating its 40th anniversary when earthquakes and tsunamis struck.
Donggala is a regency stretching along more than 300 km (180 miles) along the coast on the northestest of Sulawesi Island.
The Regency, an administrative region under the province, had a population of 299 200 in 2017.
The main components of the economics of Sulawesi province are fishing and farming, especially in the Donggala coastal region.
Nickel mining is also important in the province, but it is mainly concentrated in Morowali on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
According to the Disaster Mitigation Agency in Indonesia, Palu and Donggalu have been hit by the tsunami several times over the past 100 years.
In 1938, a tsunami in Donggale killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of homes.
Tsunami also struck the west of Donggaly in 1996, claiming nine casualties.
Indonesia is located on the Seismic Pacific Fire Circle and there are regular earthquakes.
Here's a list of several of the biggest earthquakes and tsunamis of the last few years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in the northern part of Sumatra on December 26 caused a tsunami that hit 14 countries and killed 226,000,000 people along the coast of the Indian Ocean, with more than half of them in Aceh province.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes hit the west coast of Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds of people have died on Nias Island, near Sumatra's coast.
2006: An 6.8-degree earthquake hit the south of Indonesia's most populous island of Java, causing a tsunami that crashed into the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: An 7.6-degree earthquake struck near Padang, the provincial capital of Western Sumatra.
More than 1100 people lost their lives.
2010: An earthquake of 7.5 was hit by one of Mentawai's islands, near Sumatra and caused a 10-metre tsunami that destroyed dozens of villages and killed around 300 people.
2016: A slight earthquake has struck Pidie Jay's regency in Aceh province and caused destruction and panic because it reminded people of the devastating effects of the deadly 2004 earthquake and tsunami.
This time, no tsunami formed, but more than 100 died because of falling buildings.
2018: Powerful earthquakes hit the Indonesian tourist island Lombok, killing more than 5,000 people, mostly on the north side of the island.
Earthquakes destroyed thousands of buildings, leaving thousands of tourists temporarily imprisoned.
