Federal prosecutors are demanding an exceptional death sentence for a suspect in a terrorist attack in the NYC
Federal prosecutors in New York are appealing for the death penalty for Sayfull Saipova, who is suspected of committing a terrorist attack in New York City in which eight people died – an exceptional sentence that has not been awarded for a federal crime in the state since 1953.
Saipov, 30, allegedly carried out an attack on a cycle path along the West Side Highway in the Lower Manhattan neighborhood using a rented Home Depot truck that swept away pedestrians and cyclists.
In order to defend the death penalty, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov “deliberately” killed eight victims and “deliberately” inflicted a number of injuries – thus defining the terms of intention to award the death penalty according to a document approved by the Southern District of New York.
According to judicial documents, both of these numbers would mean the possibility of awarding the death penalty.
A few weeks after the attack, a federal grand jury charged Saipov with 22 offences, such as murder charges in favor of blackmailing, which federal prosecutors routinely use in organized crime cases, and allegations of violence and the destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required "significant planning and preparation," the plaintiffs say, describing how Saip's act was carried out as heinous, cruel and twisted."
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injury, pain and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernano Ferruchi, Hernano Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damiana Pagnucco," he said.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
It's been ten years since the Southern District of New Yorking court last granted the death penalty.
Defendant Khalid Barnes was convicted of assassinating two drug suppliers but was eventually sentenced to life imprisonment in September 2009.
The last time the death penalty was actually carried out in connection with a federal case in New York in 1953, when married couple Julius and Ethel Rosenberg were executed, proving a conspiracy to spy for the Soviet Union during the Cold War two years earlier.
Both Rosenbergs sat in an electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, a native of Uzbekistan, showed minimal regret, according to judicial documents, during the days and months following the attack.
According to the police, he said he felt good because of what he had done.
According to accusations, Saipov told the authorities that he was inspired to watch Islamic State videos on his phone.
Police have also disclosed that they have also demanded putting up an Islamic State flag in a hospital room.
He said he felt innocent about the 22 charges.
David Patton, one of Saipov's federal public defenders, said they were "obviously disappointed" by the plaintiff's decision.
"We believe that the decision to demand the death penalty instead of admitting guilt and convicting to life imprisonment without the possibility of release will only prolong the trauma of these events for anyone involved," Patton said.
Saipov's team of defense attorneys has previously asked prosecutors not to demand the death penalty.
