Red tide update: Concentrations are declining in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota counties
The latest Florida Fish and Wildlife report points to an overall decline in red tide concentrations in the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC statement, stains of flowering water are now reported from Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties – indicating decreasing concentration.
Blossoming red tide water has a range of about 130 miles of coastline from the northern county of Pinellas to the southern district of Lee.
Spots were observed about 10 miles off the coast in Hillsboorghool County, but there were relatively fewer places last week.
The red tide has also been observed in Pasco county.
Last week, median concentrations were reported off the coast or on theeath near Pinellas County. In Hillsborough County, low to high concentrations were observed, the lowest-to-high concentrations in Manatee County, the lowest to high concentrations in Sarasota County, the lowest-to-high concentrations off the coast or high sea by the Lee county, and low concentrations in Collier County.
Respiratory irritation continues to occur in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
Irritation of the airway has not been reported in northwest Florida last week.
